00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:08.390
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:11.180
Welcome to the last session
of the first day of Next.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:12.940
Hope you guys have
had a good time,

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:16.760
and I hope you
enjoy this session

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.580
for our last one of the day.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.837
I am Peter-Mark Verwoerd.

00:00:20.837 --> 00:00:22.420
I'm the Head of
Migration Architecture

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:25.900
here at Google, based
out of New York.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.340
I'm going to be working with--

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.830
or I'm going to be speaking
with, beg your pardon,

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.330
Rae Wang, Jeff Allen
and Morgante Pell.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:35.470
And what we're going to do
is go over cloud foundation

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:39.980
and how to automate that
foundation in the cloud.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.770
And what that is is
a-- a cloud foundation

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:45.340
is what you need to create
before you move or create

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:48.260
a single compute
resource in the cloud.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:52.450
It's what you need to make sure
everything's working properly.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.400
I like to use the
analogy of a house

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.800
because I think it
really matches up

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.620
what you're doing in the cloud.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:01.600
If you think about it,
the cloud foundation

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.650
is what you need before you
start putting in windows,

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.840
putting in doors, painting--

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:08.660
all these things.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.810
You need a solid foundation,
you need plumbing,

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:16.072
you need wiring before you
build a house that's solid.

00:01:16.072 --> 00:01:17.530
And what we want
to do is really go

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:23.640
over what will make your
house in the cloud solid.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.820
So as you can probably
guess from my title,

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.520
I work with a lot of customers
who are moving to GCP.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.260
And when I work with
these customers,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.450
we follow a relatively
prescriptive process.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.162
And the reason we do
this is because it works.

00:01:37.162 --> 00:01:39.120
It probably doesn't seem
particularly different

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.355
from things you've seen
at other companies,

00:01:41.355 --> 00:01:46.770
but consistency around investing
in our customers in this plan

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.320
allows us to do what
we do over and over

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.090
and over again, and
repeat that success.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:54.654
So when we first engage
with customers, we come in

00:01:54.654 --> 00:01:56.070
and we figure out
what you've got.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.610
We assess the challenges,
limitations of the systems

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.200
that you have
today, and find out

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.570
what your goals, hopes,
plans, and requirements

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:04.740
are for the future.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.800
And this is how we
get, and when we plan

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.630
what you're going to migrate.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:11.715
This is before anything
is done in GCP.

00:02:11.715 --> 00:02:15.660
This is just a conversation
between us and our customers.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.730
After that, we build
the foundation.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.670
We design how we
are going to take

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.000
the solution and technologies
available in Google Cloud

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.080
and connect them to the
problems, environments,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.270
and applications
that you have today.

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.190
And then we establish
a place to build

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:36.420
all of those applications in
a secure and agile manner.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.640
And then we help
you deploy that.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:39.850
We're never going to go away.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:42.021
So we are working together
on a deployment plan

00:02:42.021 --> 00:02:44.520
to make sure it's successful,
to make sure we follow through

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.350
with the things that
we plan together,

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:49.530
that we discuss together, that
we created in the foundation

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:51.660
together, and make sure
that you have access

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.160
to technical
resources on our side

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.550
so that those systems work
how they're supposed to.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:57.985
And we work through performance.

00:02:57.985 --> 00:02:59.110
We work through management.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:01.470
We work through
operations of all of that.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:02.882
And then we're still not done.

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:04.590
Then we start talking
about optimization.

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:07.120
We start looking at, what
are the long-term plans?

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.717
What could you do
with what you have?

00:03:08.717 --> 00:03:10.050
Where would you like to improve?

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:11.970
What is not working as
well as you would like to?

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:12.960
What are the new
things that you'd

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.540
like to try out that you
haven't had a chance to try out?

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.760
And this is a process
that continues

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.899
as our relationship
with you continues.

00:03:20.899 --> 00:03:23.190
This is not something that
happens and then we're done.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:24.960
We keep on working with you.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.360
And so the optimization
is the last step,

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.100
but it is the last
step that is ongoing

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.840
throughout our relationship
with our customers.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:34.750
So today, though,
we are just going

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:39.160
to focus on that second
step, on the foundation.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.174
When I engage with
customers, I tend

00:03:41.174 --> 00:03:42.340
to harp on this quite a bit.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:44.680
Because I feel like
a solid foundation

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:47.470
really gives you the
flexibility and possibility

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:49.270
to have a successful migration.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:52.330
And that requires doing a
lot of that work upfront.

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:55.540
And so while we're
just going to focus

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:57.070
on the foundation
in this session,

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.230
there are a lot of other
sessions happening at Next

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:01.240
that you can attend
that are going

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:03.880
to cover different aspects of
the overall migration journey.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.850
And we've got a few suggestions
at the end of this session

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.480
that you might be interested in.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.360
So let's get to it.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:15.130
What we're going to cover
today is the minute detail

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:17.920
of how we're going to
build out a foundation.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:21.100
We're going to cover identity
and organization in the cloud.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.380
And then once you have that
part of the foundation,

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:27.239
then you start adding, onto
that, network and security.

00:04:27.239 --> 00:04:29.530
And so these are kind of the
very core, important parts

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:30.321
of your foundation.

00:04:30.321 --> 00:04:33.580
This is what you need to have--

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:36.220
again, what I said-- the
secure and agile place

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.792
for applications to reside.

00:04:37.792 --> 00:04:39.250
And then when we're
going to do is,

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:41.416
once we've covered all that,
we're going to show you

00:04:41.416 --> 00:04:43.540
how you could automate
that entire process,

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:48.847
and have it spun up in a
repeatable way with code

00:04:48.847 --> 00:04:50.680
that you can check in,
that you can version,

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.980
that you can build on, and
have a foundational cloud

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:55.390
that you can automate.

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:59.800
So with that, I'd like to
hand it off to my colleague,

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.800
Rae Wang, who will discuss
identity and organization.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.984
Thank you.

00:05:04.984 --> 00:05:06.948
RAE WANG: Hi, my name is Rae.

00:05:06.948 --> 00:05:10.059
I lead the product team at
GCP to work on [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:10.059 --> 00:05:11.520
and Policy Management.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.070
As Peter-Mark just said,
setting up identity,

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:17.380
organization, and
access policies

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:20.180
are the first steps
to a GCP migration.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:22.530
So let's walk through them.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:25.380
Now, you want to make sure
that the data and resources you

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.370
have on GCP are very secure.

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:29.610
So for anybody to
access them, first they

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:32.610
have to be authenticated
and authorized.

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:35.850
Authentication and authorization
work hand-in-hand, pretty much

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:38.040
like your passport and visa.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.620
Authentication
proves who you are,

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:43.800
and authorization proves
what you have access to.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.770
They are also often known
as the identity and access

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:47.790
management, IAM.

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:55.760
So for you to authenticate
to talk to GCP,

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:59.870
you have to have an identity
that's understood by GCP.

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:03.920
The same identity that you also
use to access Gmail and Docs

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:06.220
can be used to access
GCP APIs, as well.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.430
And if you are an
Enterprise customer,

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:13.830
you need to do more to
actually manage and track

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:14.950
the identities.

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:16.800
And if you're
looking for an IDP,

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.190
one great option here is
to use Cloud identity.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:21.930
Cloud identity gives
you a central place

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:26.730
to create, curate, and manage
the credentials of a company.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:28.530
If you're a GCP-only
customer, you

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:30.450
could take advantage
of cloud identities

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:33.570
without having to
sign up to G-Suite.

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:36.930
Now if you use cloud identities,
because Google manages

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:40.950
your login, you get the benefit
of secure login, password

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:45.780
reset, session device tracking,
and can know where and when

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:47.480
people have logged in.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:49.140
And because Google
tracks the logins,

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:51.660
Google can detect
suspicious activities,

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:55.420
and it can be configured to
alert you when they happen.

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:57.270
So cloud identity
is a great option

00:06:57.270 --> 00:07:00.360
if you're [INAUDIBLE] a cloud
company, don't have an existing

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:03.300
IDP, and you're looking
for a simple solution.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:06.690
But what if you already
have invested in an IDP,

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:08.520
and you have a process
that works for you,

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:11.030
you want to continue to use?

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:12.900
You can continue to
use your existing

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:17.130
IDP with GCP via Federation
and single sign-on.

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:19.530
One single sign-on,
SSO, is configured.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:23.340
Whenever one of your
identities comes to GCP,

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:26.720
they will be redirected to
your IDP for authentication.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:29.310
And because it's pretty common
that your user has already

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:31.260
been authenticated
in your own system,

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:35.270
that means they will get
instant access to GCP APIs.

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:39.920
So for SSO to work, the other
thing that needs to exist

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:43.124
is the users that
you redirect to GCP,

00:07:43.124 --> 00:07:45.290
they need to have an account
that's known to Google,

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:47.150
and already exists in Google.

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:52.280
To help with that, we have a
tool called GCDS, Google Cloud

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:54.780
Directory Sync, that will
help you to automatically

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:58.700
sync all of your users
and groups into Google.

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:02.210
Using Federation with SSO is
a very common configuration

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:04.490
used by most of our
largest customers,

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:07.070
with some syncing up to a few
hundred thousand accounts.

00:08:09.901 --> 00:08:10.900
So how do you set it up?

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:13.030
Well, setting up
SSO is very simple.

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:17.470
There's three links to your
existing identity system.

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:20.980
And SSO is built
on top of SAML 2.

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:24.460
As some of you may know, SAML 2
is the secure industry protocol

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:27.140
for exchanging user credentials.

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:29.880
And the user [INAUDIBLE] we care
about here is the user names.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:32.140
Now, SAML 2 also
offers the option

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:36.340
to digitally sign information
to prevent tampering.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:38.970
To do that, you can
provide the information

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:42.262
on the digital certificate
used to verify your signature.

00:08:42.262 --> 00:08:43.720
When you do that,
you can make sure

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:45.760
that all the information
coming through SSO

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:49.900
is indeed coming from you and
has not being tampered with.

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:52.210
And what if your system
does not support SAML 2?

00:08:52.210 --> 00:08:54.970
Well, you can also use one
of the third party options,

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:57.470
such as [INAUDIBLE],,
to bridge the gap here.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:04.570
So once you use Cloud Identity,
whether as your own IDP,

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:07.600
or whether as a
Federation tool, you also

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:11.590
get access to other
advanced features that

00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:13.360
are offered on top of GCP.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:16.500
One such example is BeyondCorp.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:20.350
BeyondCorp is an
enterprise-grade security model

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:24.100
that's based on top of the six
years of learning from building

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:26.650
zero trust network at
Google, and combined

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:30.846
with the best of breed ideas
and practices from industry.

00:09:30.846 --> 00:09:32.470
And now, BeyondCorp
is available to you

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:36.730
as a GCP service called
IDP, Identity Aware Proxy.

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:40.150
Using IDP, your administrators
can define which users

00:09:40.150 --> 00:09:42.790
or groups can-- and
under which context,

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:45.340
such as networking
secure device--

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:47.620
can access the
applications you have that

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:51.550
are running on top
of GCP, as well.

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:54.360
So with all these benefits,
how do you get Cloud Identity?

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:56.610
It's very simple to
use as a GCP customer.

00:09:56.610 --> 00:10:00.030
You go to the GCP Cloud
console, you click on Identity.

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:02.940
Here, you see a
page which has links

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:04.830
to all kinds of documentations.

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:09.090
You can click Navigate to manage
your users and groups, Google

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:10.470
Admin Console.

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:12.310
And if you click on
the Sign Up button,

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:14.040
it will take you
through a guided flow

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:19.120
to sign up for Cloud Identity
for your company's domain.

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:22.720
At the end of this
flow, a GCP organization

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:24.520
will automatically
be provisioned

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:26.230
for your Cloud Identity domain.

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:28.866
We'll talk more about
organizations a little later.

00:10:28.866 --> 00:10:30.490
And here, you will
also have the option

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:32.110
to go through guided
flows to set up

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:34.510
the rest of your
organization-related stuff,

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:37.150
such as IAM,
organization policies,

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:38.260
and billing for a company.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:44.410
So once you sign up
for Cloud Identity,

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:47.050
you have Federation set
up, your users and groups

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:49.254
will be synced into Google.

00:10:49.254 --> 00:10:50.920
And we talked about
users are important,

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:52.859
but why do you
need to use Groups?

00:10:52.859 --> 00:10:54.650
So we all know that,
in a large enterprise,

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:57.640
you might have thousands,
or even tens of thousands

00:10:57.640 --> 00:10:58.840
of users.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:00.580
It would be really
cumbersome if you

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:03.490
have to manage each individual
one of their access.

00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:06.040
So instead, we want to
manage at the group level.

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:09.970
Groups provide a level of
abstraction between your users

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:12.290
and the resources
they need to access.

00:11:12.290 --> 00:11:13.550
So let's take an example.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:18.463
If you have a Secop team, they
need the security admin role

00:11:18.463 --> 00:11:21.340
in your organization so they can
configure the security settings

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:22.690
for the organization.

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:24.337
And that team has 10 people.

00:11:24.337 --> 00:11:26.170
So what you want to do
is to create a group,

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:28.000
have these 10
members join a group,

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:29.690
and then assign a
group security admin

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:31.402
role in your organization.

00:11:31.402 --> 00:11:33.860
Now if, after that, you have
new members joining the group,

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:36.100
all you have to do is to
add a user to the group.

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:37.810
You don't have to go
and assign the user,

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:38.710
and give them the
permissions they

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:40.030
need on different resources.

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:43.540
They automatically
inherit what a group has.

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:45.790
What about if the team's
job function changes?

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:47.650
Now they also need
the log viewer role

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:52.010
so that they can watch for
critical admin setting changes.

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:53.960
All you need to do is
to assign a log viewer

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:57.067
role to the Secop team
group in your organization.

00:11:57.067 --> 00:11:58.650
Again, you won't
have to go and change

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:00.191
the setting for the
individual users.

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:07.020
You can manage groups either
through a cloud identity,

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:09.570
or in your own IDP
via Federation.

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:11.370
But in either case,
you want to make sure

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:14.210
that you safeguard your
group membership by setting

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:16.440
additional policies,
such as the policy

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:19.500
to prevent outside identities
from joining your company's

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:22.410
domain groups, or the
permissions on who

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:26.760
is able to create new
groups of their own.

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:28.680
So we talked about
users and groups.

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:30.390
There is a third
type of identity

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:33.600
that's often used with GCP,
which is service accounts.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:35.570
So unlike users groups,
which are identities

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:37.800
for human beings,
your service accounts

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:40.680
are the identities for your
automations and programs

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:44.050
that are running on top of GCP.

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:46.706
Your users can type the
user and password to log in.

00:12:46.706 --> 00:12:48.330
The service account
is not going to sit

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:50.020
in front of a screen type.

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:52.684
So instead, they use
keys to authenticate.

00:12:52.684 --> 00:12:55.350
One or more service account keys
can be created for each service

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:56.252
account.

00:12:56.252 --> 00:12:57.960
And since using these
keys, you basically

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:00.240
are granting people access
to your sensitive data

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:01.950
and resources, you
want to make sure

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:04.200
that you safeguard the keys.

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:06.240
If you're running
applications on top of Google

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:10.410
with GAE, GCE, or GKE, we
will keep your keys secure,

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:13.140
and then automatically
rotate them every few hours.

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:15.390
So that way, you don't
have to download keys,

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:18.877
and you don't have to risk
losing or leaking your keys.

00:13:18.877 --> 00:13:20.460
What if you run
applications elsewhere

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:21.710
in a hybrid cloud environment?

00:13:21.710 --> 00:13:23.100
That happens quite often.

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:24.750
Well, you can download
the keys to be

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:26.874
used from your applications
so your application can

00:13:26.874 --> 00:13:28.260
access GCP.

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:31.380
However, here, you really
need to be careful about where

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:32.520
you store those keys.

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:34.840
They give access
to sensitive data,

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:37.560
so you want to store them in a
secure service, such as maybe

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:39.630
a vault. And what
you don't want to do

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:43.069
is just to store them
in plain text on GitHub.

00:13:43.069 --> 00:13:45.110
The other interesting
thing about service account

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:48.440
is that it's not only an
identity, it's also a resource.

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:51.560
So when you have a VM that's
running as a service account,

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:53.150
here, the service
account is acting

00:13:53.150 --> 00:13:56.750
as the identity-- the VM
assumes the service account

00:13:56.750 --> 00:14:01.010
identity to get access
to resources and APIs.

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:02.750
However, at the
same time, you also

00:14:02.750 --> 00:14:05.990
want to be able to control
which users, which VMs,

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:08.370
are able to assume
this identity.

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:11.322
So here, the service
account acts as a resource,

00:14:11.322 --> 00:14:13.280
and you can grant access
to the service account

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:15.710
by granting the service
account [? act as ?] role.

00:14:18.590 --> 00:14:22.790
Now, a very common way of using
service account is with GCVMs.

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:26.330
When you use a service
account to run GCVMs,

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:28.760
you can subpartition all
the VMs in your project

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:31.760
into different components
with different identities.

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:34.550
For example, if you
have one component that

00:14:34.550 --> 00:14:36.230
needs to access a
[INAUDIBLE] bucket,

00:14:36.230 --> 00:14:37.730
and a different
component that needs

00:14:37.730 --> 00:14:40.370
to access a big
query data set, you

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:42.230
can assign each one
a different identity,

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:46.670
and then grant each identity
the least privilege required.

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:48.500
You can change the
service account on VM

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:50.354
without having to restart a VM.

00:14:50.354 --> 00:14:51.770
And later, in the
network section,

00:14:51.770 --> 00:14:52.880
we're going to talk
about how to use

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:54.546
these attributes of
the service accounts

00:14:54.546 --> 00:14:56.570
to create more secure
firewall rules.

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:03.850
So once you have your identity's
domain set up with GCP,

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:07.330
an organization is
automatically created for you.

00:15:07.330 --> 00:15:11.170
The GCP organization is the
root node for all the resources

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:12.260
in your company.

00:15:12.260 --> 00:15:14.400
So all of your VMs,
buckets in the projects,

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:17.135
and so on live under
the organization.

00:15:17.135 --> 00:15:19.510
There's a 1-to-1 relationship
between the identity domain

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:21.514
and the GCP organization.

00:15:21.514 --> 00:15:22.930
And using this
relationship, we're

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:26.110
able to enforce rules such
that all the projects,

00:15:26.110 --> 00:15:28.810
and the resources that are
created by your company's

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:31.720
employees, will belong to
your organization node.

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:33.640
And this ensures
that there's no more

00:15:33.640 --> 00:15:35.650
[INAUDIBLE] or hidden projects.

00:15:35.650 --> 00:15:38.590
The central admin will
have control and visibility

00:15:38.590 --> 00:15:41.570
to everything.

00:15:41.570 --> 00:15:43.230
So more about the
resource hierarchy.

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:44.730
Well, we talked
about you don't want

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:46.200
to manage individual users.

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:48.740
You also don't want to manage
individual VMs and storage

00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:49.425
objects, right?

00:15:49.425 --> 00:15:51.300
You're going to end up
with thousands of them

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:53.470
and, in the case of
objects, millions of them.

00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:55.520
They're cattle, not pets.

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:57.260
So to scale to
operation, you want

00:15:57.260 --> 00:16:00.950
to be able to run operations and
set policies on a group level.

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:04.190
And the most common way of
grouping resources in GCP

00:16:04.190 --> 00:16:06.450
is through the
research hierarchy.

00:16:06.450 --> 00:16:09.080
At the bottom level, you have
your VMs and storage objects,

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:10.880
and then buckets.

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:12.760
Above that, you have projects.

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:17.090
Projects are the most basic
unit for ownership and policies

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:18.440
in GCP.

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:21.530
They are often used
to assimilate a team

00:16:21.530 --> 00:16:23.820
or environment or service.

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:25.706
And about projects,
you have folders.

00:16:25.706 --> 00:16:27.080
They're hierarchical
and they can

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:29.270
be used to model your
departments or organization

00:16:29.270 --> 00:16:32.010
units.

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:34.510
And then, above the folders,
you have the organization node,

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:36.830
which is, again, your root node.

00:16:36.830 --> 00:16:39.110
So once you have set up
your resource hierarchy,

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:42.220
it makes a lot of the
organization-wide management

00:16:42.220 --> 00:16:44.000
tasks much simpler.

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:46.000
For example, you can
control who in your company

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:48.100
is or is not allowed
to start new projects

00:16:48.100 --> 00:16:49.840
and create additional resources.

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:52.330
You can do so by assigning the
project creator role at each

00:16:52.330 --> 00:16:54.550
of the levels in a hierarchy.

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:56.680
You can also
automate the building

00:16:56.680 --> 00:16:59.230
of an inventory of your
resources and policies

00:16:59.230 --> 00:17:02.950
by programmatically traversing
through the org, the folders,

00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:05.540
projects, their
resources, and so on.

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:07.300
Another common
practice is to assign

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:10.403
a service account, the
permissions on the org level.

00:17:10.403 --> 00:17:12.069
So the service account
can go and patrol

00:17:12.069 --> 00:17:14.859
the configurations
in your organization

00:17:14.859 --> 00:17:16.599
to enforce compliance.

00:17:16.599 --> 00:17:19.230
And in fact, this is what
Google's own security

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:21.781
team does to manage our
Google.com GCP resources,

00:17:21.781 --> 00:17:22.280
as well.

00:17:24.889 --> 00:17:26.180
So resource hierarchy is great.

00:17:26.180 --> 00:17:28.660
It defines a very strict
ownership hierarchy,

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:30.750
and it gives you
policy inheritance.

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:32.260
But there many
times you also need

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:33.760
to define other
relationships that

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:35.770
are outside of the hierarchy.

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:37.930
For example, you can
have two departments.

00:17:37.930 --> 00:17:41.770
You have a storefront team
and an inventory service,

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:43.870
each of which can
have a production

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:46.360
environment versus a
dev testing environment.

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:48.370
How do you define
each environment?

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:51.190
One kind of a way of
doing that is by labels.

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:54.160
Labels are key value pairs
that are user-defined and can

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:56.450
be attached to resources.

00:17:56.450 --> 00:17:58.330
So in this case, you
can assign environment

00:17:58.330 --> 00:18:03.010
equal to prod versus dev to the
different projects in the two

00:18:03.010 --> 00:18:04.300
different departments.

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:05.920
Once you have
assigned labels, you

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:09.610
can search for resources
based on your labels.

00:18:09.610 --> 00:18:12.130
And labels are also
included in billing export,

00:18:12.130 --> 00:18:14.050
so you can use that to
do a cost aggregation

00:18:14.050 --> 00:18:18.770
for your different
efforts and initiatives.

00:18:18.770 --> 00:18:21.440
So once you have the resource
hierarchy and labels,

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:24.530
we can also define
hierarchical policies.

00:18:24.530 --> 00:18:28.290
Some examples here-- IAM,
Identity Access Management--

00:18:28.290 --> 00:18:30.650
organization policies,
which is about constraining

00:18:30.650 --> 00:18:35.610
the configurations in a
company, and hierarchical quota.

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:40.170
So IAM is the policy that
allows you to tell who

00:18:40.170 --> 00:18:42.480
can do what on which resources.

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:45.180
The who part refers
to identities.

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:47.070
And the resource
part can be defined

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:48.785
by either individual
resources, like VMs

00:18:48.785 --> 00:18:51.390
or buckets, or
hierarchical nodes,

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:53.470
such as projects and folders.

00:18:53.470 --> 00:18:55.200
The "can do what"
part, in the middle,

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:57.900
is defined by an IM role.

00:18:57.900 --> 00:19:00.310
An IM role often maps
to a job function.

00:19:00.310 --> 00:19:04.350
For example, if you are the
instance admin for a team.

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:08.400
And it is constructed as
a group of permissions.

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:10.400
So here, if you need to
manage all the instances

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:12.941
for a department, you need to
be able to get in [INAUDIBLE],,

00:19:12.941 --> 00:19:14.620
start and stop them, and so on.

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:16.230
So we group these
permissions together

00:19:16.230 --> 00:19:18.186
and make them an IM role.

00:19:18.186 --> 00:19:19.560
In this example
here, you can see

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:21.185
that we're assigning
the instance admin

00:19:21.185 --> 00:19:24.780
role to a number of
users on project A, which

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:26.730
means these users
can then use the role

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:31.050
to manage, to run operations
on instances in this project.

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:33.660
Now, GCP ships, out
of the box, over a 100

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:35.690
curated roles that are
being built for you.

00:19:35.690 --> 00:19:38.820
Hopefully, it will map to a
lot of your job functions.

00:19:38.820 --> 00:19:41.720
But if you need more, you
can also build custom roles

00:19:41.720 --> 00:19:48.010
to specifically tailor roles
that match your business needs.

00:19:48.010 --> 00:19:50.289
So another new capability
to IM that we're

00:19:50.289 --> 00:19:52.330
going to be launching in
the next few days-- this

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:55.330
is a sneak preview--
is IM Conditions.

00:19:55.330 --> 00:19:59.590
IM Conditions allows you to
further restrict the access

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:03.250
to IM grants by additional,
either client or resource,

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:04.490
attributes.

00:20:04.490 --> 00:20:08.122
So for example, I can assign
Peter-Mark storage admin

00:20:08.122 --> 00:20:09.580
on a bucket, but
with the condition

00:20:09.580 --> 00:20:11.560
that he can only
access the objects that

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:13.700
match a particular prefix.

00:20:13.700 --> 00:20:17.080
So you can grant
subpacket-level IM that way.

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:21.190
You can also use conditions
to do context aware access.

00:20:21.190 --> 00:20:22.840
For example, you can
assign permission

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:25.450
to access particularly
sensitive resources,

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:28.630
but only when the client
traffic is coming from a trusted

00:20:28.630 --> 00:20:30.880
network, maybe a company's VPN.

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:32.380
And later on in the
network section,

00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:33.796
we're also going
to talk about how

00:20:33.796 --> 00:20:36.550
to use context
aware with your VPC

00:20:36.550 --> 00:20:40.470
to set the boundary at your
organization level, as well.

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:43.300
Another common use of conditions
is to set time-bound access

00:20:43.300 --> 00:20:44.380
policies.

00:20:44.380 --> 00:20:46.270
So you can grant
temporary access

00:20:46.270 --> 00:20:48.840
and have it auto-expire
when time is up.

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:50.590
This is often used,
for example, if you're

00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:52.930
an admin, for the
break glass situation,

00:20:52.930 --> 00:20:55.120
or to time with your
team's on-call rotations.

00:20:57.920 --> 00:21:00.680
So IM policy focuses on
the I, on the identity.

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:03.800
It differentiates access
based on who you are.

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:06.320
It's sibling,
organization policy,

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:09.770
allows the admin to set secure
configuration constraints

00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:11.210
for the organization.

00:21:11.210 --> 00:21:13.400
It differentiates
based on resources

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:14.830
and applies to
all the identities

00:21:14.830 --> 00:21:17.690
that access a
particular resource.

00:21:17.690 --> 00:21:20.480
Organization constraints
can be set on the org level,

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:23.150
and it will be inherited
into all of the projects

00:21:23.150 --> 00:21:24.620
and resources underneath.

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:27.245
And it can also be either
augmented or overridden

00:21:27.245 --> 00:21:32.520
on the other organization level,
such as folders and projects.

00:21:32.520 --> 00:21:35.160
So here's some examples
of the organization policy

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:36.640
that are available today.

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:39.480
You can, for example,
limit the usage

00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:43.710
of VM nested virtualization
or external IP serial port.

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:46.180
You can also use it to
control your IM policies.

00:21:46.180 --> 00:21:49.170
For example, preventing
external identities

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:52.970
from being added to the access
of your company's resources,

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:55.935
or prevent the creation of
service accounts or keys

00:21:55.935 --> 00:21:59.230
in a particular project.

00:21:59.230 --> 00:22:02.670
So let's look at one of them
in more details, the restricted

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:04.860
VM trusted image policy.

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:06.600
So if you use GCE,
you might already

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:09.990
be aware of the GCE image
sharing feature, which

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:13.290
allows you to curate your
images under one project,

00:22:13.290 --> 00:22:16.860
and share it with the other
VMs in the other projects.

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:20.070
Now, once you've set up the
corresponding org policies,

00:22:20.070 --> 00:22:23.050
you can whitelist a
set of projects hosting

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:25.380
the VMs that are
allowed to be used

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:28.200
at a safe for your organization.

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:30.390
For example, you can limit
it to only the VMs that

00:22:30.390 --> 00:22:33.750
are published by Google, or
only the projects containing

00:22:33.750 --> 00:22:38.470
VMs that have been fully tested
and blessed by a security team.

00:22:38.470 --> 00:22:41.295
And once you have configured
this policy, the next time

00:22:41.295 --> 00:22:43.410
your developers come and
try to [INAUDIBLE] VM,

00:22:43.410 --> 00:22:47.304
they will only be able to
choose from these safe images

00:22:47.304 --> 00:22:48.720
that you have
defined by a policy.

00:22:51.231 --> 00:22:51.730
OK.

00:22:51.730 --> 00:22:52.479
So that was a lot.

00:22:52.479 --> 00:22:56.140
Let's put them together and look
an end-to-end example of how

00:22:56.140 --> 00:22:59.490
to configure identities,
organization,

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:02.381
and acess policies for
an enterprise company.

00:23:02.381 --> 00:23:03.880
So if you run an
enterprise company,

00:23:03.880 --> 00:23:05.650
you probably have
multiple levels

00:23:05.650 --> 00:23:09.010
of organizations that are
built up over the years.

00:23:09.010 --> 00:23:10.480
In this day and
age, you probably

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:13.930
care about security and
privacy and separation of duty.

00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:17.260
And you likely also already
have existing on-prem assets

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:19.360
and processes that
your cloud deployments

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:21.530
need to be able to work with.

00:23:21.530 --> 00:23:23.440
So this is a typical setup.

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:25.570
You might have your
on-prem [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:28.300
system hosting identities,
and then Federate them

00:23:28.300 --> 00:23:31.537
into Google via Cloud Identity.

00:23:31.537 --> 00:23:33.370
And an organization,
though, would be set up

00:23:33.370 --> 00:23:35.980
to match your Cloud
Identity account,

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:39.640
and would give your admins
central visibility and control

00:23:39.640 --> 00:23:41.800
of all your resources.

00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:43.600
Organization policies
can be set up

00:23:43.600 --> 00:23:46.840
to prevent unsafe configurations
for your organization,

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:51.280
and protect compliance
and security requirements.

00:23:51.280 --> 00:23:54.280
And it is often that
procurement decisions

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:56.830
are made at the org
level centrally,

00:23:56.830 --> 00:23:58.420
instead of every
developer deciding

00:23:58.420 --> 00:23:59.980
what to use on their own.

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:03.700
And then, resource provisioning,
as well as major configuration

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:06.340
changes, are driven
through existing processes,

00:24:06.340 --> 00:24:09.950
such as a ticketing
system or a CICD pipeline.

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:12.670
Now, the benefit of doing
that is that your developers

00:24:12.670 --> 00:24:16.430
won't need to have direct
access to the actual resources,

00:24:16.430 --> 00:24:18.470
especially in a
production environment.

00:24:18.470 --> 00:24:22.020
Instead, they can request
that all the changes be driven

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:24.699
through existing
automated workflows,

00:24:24.699 --> 00:24:27.240
and that everything will leave
a trace through your git-repo,

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:30.190
your CICD pipeline.

00:24:30.190 --> 00:24:32.380
And then you can set up
your folders and projects

00:24:32.380 --> 00:24:35.590
to model your teams and
departments so that they

00:24:35.590 --> 00:24:38.500
can have very separate policies,
as well as different cost

00:24:38.500 --> 00:24:40.010
allocation.

00:24:40.010 --> 00:24:42.280
So if you have your HR
department with their apps,

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:44.890
and your storefront
department with their apps,

00:24:44.890 --> 00:24:48.160
you can make sure that they can
each get very different access

00:24:48.160 --> 00:24:51.394
levels and compliance policies.

00:24:51.394 --> 00:24:52.810
And when it comes
to billing time,

00:24:52.810 --> 00:24:57.400
you can also figure out how
much each department has spent.

00:24:57.400 --> 00:24:59.650
So that was a quick
tour through setting up

00:24:59.650 --> 00:25:03.420
identities, organization, access
policies for your organization.

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:05.590
Another important
step in your migration

00:25:05.590 --> 00:25:08.530
will be figuring out how to
configure network settings.

00:25:08.530 --> 00:25:12.670
So for that, let's welcome
on stage Jeff Allen.

00:25:12.670 --> 00:25:16.470
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:18.845 --> 00:25:20.470
JEFF ALLEN: Hi, I'm Jeff Allen.

00:25:20.470 --> 00:25:22.890
I'm a Solutions Architect
here at Google Cloud.

00:25:22.890 --> 00:25:26.490
And what we're going
to talk about is--

00:25:26.490 --> 00:25:30.810
building on this conversation
of identity and organization--

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:33.720
some of the foundations and some
of the foundational decisions

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:37.110
we need to make in the area
of network and security,

00:25:37.110 --> 00:25:39.990
focusing really on
three primary topics--

00:25:39.990 --> 00:25:42.120
the typology, the
structure, and the layout

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:45.420
of our network, as well
as network connectivity.

00:25:45.420 --> 00:25:48.700
As we design this network to
interact with other systems,

00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:51.160
how are we going to
create that connectivity?

00:25:51.160 --> 00:25:52.860
And in regards to
security, how are we

00:25:52.860 --> 00:25:55.290
going to do access
control and auditing

00:25:55.290 --> 00:26:01.570
and have visibility into what's
going on in those environments?

00:26:01.570 --> 00:26:03.960
So let's start this
typology discussion

00:26:03.960 --> 00:26:07.080
by looking at the Google
Cloud global network.

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:09.810
Now, you may have seen
this depiction before,

00:26:09.810 --> 00:26:11.520
where we've got
all of our regions

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:13.320
up here, along with
the points of presence,

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:17.010
where you can ingress onto
the Google private network.

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:18.870
And all the lines
connecting the dots

00:26:18.870 --> 00:26:22.290
are the miles of our
private fiber sub-sea cables

00:26:22.290 --> 00:26:24.910
that really create what
differentiates Google Cloud

00:26:24.910 --> 00:26:28.230
Platform, this global network
with very low latency,

00:26:28.230 --> 00:26:31.150
single-hop routing
around the globe.

00:26:31.150 --> 00:26:33.210
And it's this network
which provides

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:37.367
the backbone for your global
VPCs on Google Cloud Platform.

00:26:37.367 --> 00:26:39.450
Now, if you're interested
in this map, by the way,

00:26:39.450 --> 00:26:43.230
I will call out that you can
get a copy of it, a poster,

00:26:43.230 --> 00:26:44.860
if you're interested.

00:26:44.860 --> 00:26:49.920
So if you go to this link,
g.co/poster, in the US,

00:26:49.920 --> 00:26:53.430
we'll mail you a free copy
of that wall-sized poster.

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:57.222
And if you're not, you can
still download a high res image

00:26:57.222 --> 00:26:59.430
that you could use with your
own large format printer

00:26:59.430 --> 00:27:00.850
to print your own poster.

00:27:00.850 --> 00:27:03.517
So if you want to have one of
those hanging on your wall,

00:27:03.517 --> 00:27:04.350
check out this link.

00:27:07.390 --> 00:27:09.390
So it's within
that global network

00:27:09.390 --> 00:27:12.300
where we define our VPCs,
our Virtual Private Clouds.

00:27:12.300 --> 00:27:14.580
Those are the network
environments where

00:27:14.580 --> 00:27:15.910
we'll launch our resources.

00:27:15.910 --> 00:27:17.760
But the resources
themselves tend

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:20.160
to exist in what
we call subnets.

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:22.770
And the subnets aren't global,
they are regional resources.

00:27:22.770 --> 00:27:24.810
So we define subnets
in each region

00:27:24.810 --> 00:27:29.430
where we intend to maintain
some kind of a compute presence.

00:27:29.430 --> 00:27:34.586
The subnets span the zones
within those regions.

00:27:34.586 --> 00:27:36.210
But here's where we
have some decisions

00:27:36.210 --> 00:27:38.520
to make about the
topology of our network.

00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:40.120
How big do our
subnets need to be?

00:27:40.120 --> 00:27:42.030
How many subnets do we need?

00:27:42.030 --> 00:27:44.410
In what regions do we want
those subnets to exist?

00:27:44.410 --> 00:27:48.150
So we've got to consider our
own organization-specific

00:27:48.150 --> 00:27:50.537
requirements to make
those decisions.

00:27:50.537 --> 00:27:52.620
But there are some best
practices to keep in mind.

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:54.330
Certainly, we want
to have subnets

00:27:54.330 --> 00:27:57.630
available in each of the regions
where we intend our teams

00:27:57.630 --> 00:27:59.910
to maintain compute presence.

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:01.980
They'll need to be
sized appropriately,

00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:02.880
considering growth.

00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:04.800
But they're easily
extended, if you

00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:08.688
need to add additional
address space to a subnet.

00:28:14.630 --> 00:28:17.180
As we consider how to connect
these networks with our other

00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:20.000
networks-- a topic we'll
look at momentarily--

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:22.970
that relies, in general, on
having non-overlapping IP

00:28:22.970 --> 00:28:23.840
address ranges.

00:28:23.840 --> 00:28:27.360
So we want to work within
our own addressing scheme.

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:32.040
Allocate some subnets
specific to GCP.

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:34.390
So when you start connecting
this back on-prem,

00:28:34.390 --> 00:28:37.490
you have non-overlapping
address spaces to use.

00:28:37.490 --> 00:28:39.860
And also, consider
drawing parameters

00:28:39.860 --> 00:28:41.600
around the network
segments where

00:28:41.600 --> 00:28:44.000
you may want to control
which segments have access

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:45.660
to the internet.

00:28:45.660 --> 00:28:48.500
Which segment segments
are internal only?

00:28:48.500 --> 00:28:51.560
Which segments maybe have
some special protection

00:28:51.560 --> 00:28:53.090
because of some
compliance framework

00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:55.030
that were audited against?

00:28:55.030 --> 00:28:58.460
And divide up our environment
into these segments.

00:28:58.460 --> 00:29:00.410
And we'll look, throughout
this presentation,

00:29:00.410 --> 00:29:05.000
at how we can use network
routes and firewalls to create

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:11.140
those segments within
our environment.

00:29:11.140 --> 00:29:13.500
Another consideration
regarding network

00:29:13.500 --> 00:29:18.630
topology is the relationship
of our networks to our project

00:29:18.630 --> 00:29:19.340
structure.

00:29:19.340 --> 00:29:21.450
Rae just showed us
how these projects

00:29:21.450 --> 00:29:25.920
exist within a hierarchy of
folders in our organization.

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:30.300
Now, the VPC is a resource
that's owned by a project,

00:29:30.300 --> 00:29:33.870
and you certainly can have
your VM instances and your VPC

00:29:33.870 --> 00:29:36.727
network in the same
project, if you choose.

00:29:36.727 --> 00:29:39.060
But in many cases, because
of this desire for separation

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:41.640
of duties, that's
not the way we tend

00:29:41.640 --> 00:29:44.700
to see enterprise customers
deploying their networks.

00:29:44.700 --> 00:29:46.860
There's this feature
called Shared VPC,

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:50.550
which allows you to define
what we call a host project.

00:29:50.550 --> 00:29:53.850
And that host project is the
one that owns the network.

00:29:53.850 --> 00:29:56.440
This may be owned by a
network security team.

00:29:56.440 --> 00:30:00.120
They define the network, the
subnets, the firewall rules,

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:03.900
and then they share access to
what we call service projects.

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:05.280
And these service
projects may be

00:30:05.280 --> 00:30:08.440
owned by the individual
application workload teams.

00:30:08.440 --> 00:30:12.120
They're allowed to launch
resources into the shared VPC,

00:30:12.120 --> 00:30:14.370
but they themselves
don't have access

00:30:14.370 --> 00:30:17.595
to configure the network itself.

00:30:23.760 --> 00:30:26.120
Now that we've got
an idea of the way

00:30:26.120 --> 00:30:28.456
we're going to lay
out our subnets,

00:30:28.456 --> 00:30:30.830
we want to start thinking
about how the resources located

00:30:30.830 --> 00:30:33.770
inside those subnets
are going to interact

00:30:33.770 --> 00:30:36.440
with other resources
in other networks.

00:30:36.440 --> 00:30:39.800
Now, in this scenario,
we're interested in having

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:44.120
our resources in our VPC subnets
interacting with resources that

00:30:44.120 --> 00:30:45.864
may exist in a
different VPC, maybe

00:30:45.864 --> 00:30:47.780
within our same project
or a different project

00:30:47.780 --> 00:30:52.190
within our org, or
maybe even a VPC that's

00:30:52.190 --> 00:30:54.850
owned by one of our partners.

00:30:54.850 --> 00:30:55.850
It may not even be ours.

00:30:55.850 --> 00:30:58.970
But if both parties
agree to establish

00:30:58.970 --> 00:31:00.620
this peering
connection, then you

00:31:00.620 --> 00:31:03.680
can have seamless
routing over the RFC 1918

00:31:03.680 --> 00:31:06.590
addresses between
these two VPCs,

00:31:06.590 --> 00:31:08.840
allowing resources in
one VPC to communicate

00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:11.300
with resources in the other.

00:31:11.300 --> 00:31:14.750
It's one way to establish
that kind of connectivity.

00:31:18.110 --> 00:31:19.640
But what if we
want the resources

00:31:19.640 --> 00:31:24.680
in our VPC to be able to
communicate with Google APIs?

00:31:24.680 --> 00:31:27.470
For instance, Google
Cloud Storage or BigQuery.

00:31:27.470 --> 00:31:29.030
We've got instances
and we want them

00:31:29.030 --> 00:31:31.640
to be able to interact
with those APIs.

00:31:31.640 --> 00:31:35.540
Well, if we provide an
external, public, routable IP

00:31:35.540 --> 00:31:37.730
address, and a route through
our internet gateway,

00:31:37.730 --> 00:31:39.860
certainly, those
instances could egress

00:31:39.860 --> 00:31:42.230
to the internet and interact
with the Google APIs.

00:31:42.230 --> 00:31:46.160
But in many cases, we don't want
instances egressing directly

00:31:46.160 --> 00:31:47.460
to the internet.

00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:49.730
So how can we provide
access to Google APIs

00:31:49.730 --> 00:31:51.950
without internet connectivity?

00:31:51.950 --> 00:31:55.640
Private Google access
provides us a way to do that.

00:31:55.640 --> 00:31:59.480
This is a subnet-level
setting, and when it's enabled,

00:31:59.480 --> 00:32:01.400
the instances in that subnet--

00:32:01.400 --> 00:32:03.290
even without an
external IP address--

00:32:03.290 --> 00:32:06.230
can egress through the
internet gateway, specifically

00:32:06.230 --> 00:32:09.080
the Google APIs, not
the internet at large.

00:32:09.080 --> 00:32:11.690
So this is a way of
providing connectivity

00:32:11.690 --> 00:32:14.750
between your instances
and Google APIs

00:32:14.750 --> 00:32:16.640
without the instances
actually having

00:32:16.640 --> 00:32:19.062
full internet connectivity.

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:24.570
But in many cases, we
do want those instances

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:30.216
to be able to communicate
with the internet.

00:32:30.216 --> 00:32:31.590
And one way of
accomplishing that

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:34.900
without providing each
individual instance a publicly

00:32:34.900 --> 00:32:37.590
routable IP address is to route
their traffic through what

00:32:37.590 --> 00:32:40.270
we call a NAT gateway.

00:32:40.270 --> 00:32:42.720
Now, a NAT gateway
is just an instance

00:32:42.720 --> 00:32:46.240
that itself is configured with
an internet-routable address,

00:32:46.240 --> 00:32:48.550
bootstrapped to forward traffic.

00:32:48.550 --> 00:32:50.160
And then you can
figure that instance

00:32:50.160 --> 00:32:54.390
as the next top
address for the routing

00:32:54.390 --> 00:32:59.640
of internet-bound traffic
from within your VPC.

00:32:59.640 --> 00:33:02.850
You could do a single-NAT
gateway, but in many cases,

00:33:02.850 --> 00:33:06.300
concerns for resiliency
and greater bandwidth

00:33:06.300 --> 00:33:10.620
would have us deploy multiple
NAT gateways, like we see here.

00:33:10.620 --> 00:33:13.080
Where we might deploy three
NAT gateways, one in each

00:33:13.080 --> 00:33:18.190
zone within the region,
where our subnet exists.

00:33:18.190 --> 00:33:21.210
Each of these NAT gateways is
deployed in a managed instance

00:33:21.210 --> 00:33:22.430
group with health checks.

00:33:22.430 --> 00:33:24.763
So they'll be health checked,
and if those health checks

00:33:24.763 --> 00:33:28.860
start to fail, that
instance will be replaced.

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:31.656
And additionally, when
we configure the routing

00:33:31.656 --> 00:33:33.030
through these NAT
gateways, we're

00:33:33.030 --> 00:33:36.690
going to configure them
all with an equal priority.

00:33:36.690 --> 00:33:40.830
And that's going to allow us
to leverage ECMP, or Equal-Cost

00:33:40.830 --> 00:33:44.880
Multi-Path routing, which
is going to distribute

00:33:44.880 --> 00:33:48.870
our outbound traffic across
the aggregate bandwidth of all

00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:49.920
of these NAT instances.

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:51.700
So it becomes
horizontally scalable.

00:33:51.700 --> 00:33:54.540
If you need more bandwidth
to egress the internet,

00:33:54.540 --> 00:33:56.880
you just add
additional NAT gateways

00:33:56.880 --> 00:33:57.840
with the same priority.

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:06.810
Now, what if we
want connectivity

00:34:06.810 --> 00:34:10.469
not to the internet, but to
resources that exist on-prem?

00:34:10.469 --> 00:34:11.624
So data center resources.

00:34:11.624 --> 00:34:15.070
We want to create connectivity
between our VPC and the data

00:34:15.070 --> 00:34:15.570
center.

00:34:15.570 --> 00:34:17.429
There's a number
of ways to do it.

00:34:17.429 --> 00:34:19.739
We've got a VPN solution.

00:34:19.739 --> 00:34:21.670
Layer 3 peering solutions.

00:34:21.670 --> 00:34:24.210
Note, there is no SLA for
those, so in many cases,

00:34:24.210 --> 00:34:28.069
we recommend layer 2
interconnect solutions.

00:34:28.069 --> 00:34:29.610
And there's this
decision tree, which

00:34:29.610 --> 00:34:32.670
can be useful to help us
evaluate which of these five

00:34:32.670 --> 00:34:35.130
options are appropriate for us.

00:34:35.130 --> 00:34:36.840
On the left-hand
side of the decision

00:34:36.840 --> 00:34:39.300
tree is where we're exploring
the peering options.

00:34:39.300 --> 00:34:41.760
In many cases, these
are used in cases

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:46.320
where we want to establish
low-latency interactions

00:34:46.320 --> 00:34:50.070
for the G Suite APIs, for our
end users of G Suite, Google

00:34:50.070 --> 00:34:53.070
Docs, and Google Sheets.

00:34:53.070 --> 00:34:55.500
So for the purposes of
today's presentation,

00:34:55.500 --> 00:34:57.930
we're really going to focus
more on the right-hand side

00:34:57.930 --> 00:35:01.050
of this decision tree, which is
going to help us decide, well,

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:03.270
is Cloud VPN appropriate?

00:35:03.270 --> 00:35:06.480
Or maybe a full 10-gigabit,
dedicated interconnect?

00:35:06.480 --> 00:35:08.530
Or if we don't need 10
gigabits of bandwidth,

00:35:08.530 --> 00:35:11.220
maybe just the fractional
bandwidth of a partner

00:35:11.220 --> 00:35:14.040
interconnect.

00:35:14.040 --> 00:35:17.100
But we'll also see momentarily
how you might use some of these

00:35:17.100 --> 00:35:18.270
together.

00:35:18.270 --> 00:35:20.580
You might start with
a cloud VPN and then

00:35:20.580 --> 00:35:23.730
move to an
interconnect-related solution.

00:35:23.730 --> 00:35:27.960
And I'll show you a common
pathway along that journey

00:35:27.960 --> 00:35:33.270
from Cloud VPN to interconnect.

00:35:33.270 --> 00:35:35.370
So what is cloud VPN?

00:35:35.370 --> 00:35:39.810
Cloud VPN is our IPSec
connectivity option,

00:35:39.810 --> 00:35:46.176
where you create a Cloud router
in GCP and a VPN gateway.

00:35:46.176 --> 00:35:47.550
And then you
configure any number

00:35:47.550 --> 00:35:49.710
of tunnels between
that VPN gateway

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:50.965
and your on-prem routers.

00:35:53.580 --> 00:35:56.670
This allows you to have
RC 1918 connectivity.

00:35:56.670 --> 00:35:59.520
It's encrypted
because of the IPSec.

00:35:59.520 --> 00:36:01.701
And it also allows you
to provide resilience

00:36:01.701 --> 00:36:04.200
in your environment because you
can define multiple tunnels.

00:36:04.200 --> 00:36:05.574
If you have multiple
routers, you

00:36:05.574 --> 00:36:07.780
can define a tunnel to each.

00:36:07.780 --> 00:36:11.410
You can also use ECMP again
if you need a higher bandwidth

00:36:11.410 --> 00:36:11.910
solution.

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:17.370
You can expect between 1.5
and 3 gigabits per tunnel.

00:36:17.370 --> 00:36:19.350
But if you need
additional bandwidth,

00:36:19.350 --> 00:36:22.110
you can configure multiple
tunnels with equal priorities

00:36:22.110 --> 00:36:24.480
in their routing configuration,
which will, again,

00:36:24.480 --> 00:36:27.780
cause a routing to
distribute the traffic

00:36:27.780 --> 00:36:30.105
across the aggregate
bandwidth of those tunnels.

00:36:34.910 --> 00:36:37.150
So the other option that
we're going to focus on here

00:36:37.150 --> 00:36:39.650
is dedicated interconnect.

00:36:39.650 --> 00:36:42.460
Now, this is a physical link
in a colo facility, where

00:36:42.460 --> 00:36:45.010
you have a router,
Google has a router,

00:36:45.010 --> 00:36:47.260
we cross-connect those routers.

00:36:47.260 --> 00:36:50.410
And you can get a
full 10-gigabit link

00:36:50.410 --> 00:36:53.370
between your environments.

00:36:53.370 --> 00:37:00.310
We've got dedicated interconnect
locations around the globe.

00:37:00.310 --> 00:37:01.560
And you can check the website.

00:37:01.560 --> 00:37:06.930
We're frequently adding
additional locations.

00:37:06.930 --> 00:37:09.420
But remember we said that
interconnect is, in many cases,

00:37:09.420 --> 00:37:13.530
preferred because there are
supported configurations

00:37:13.530 --> 00:37:17.160
with interconnect where
there's an availability SLA.

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:23.130
So let's take a look at a couple
of those configuration options.

00:37:23.130 --> 00:37:30.330
So this would be the dedicated
interconnect configuration

00:37:30.330 --> 00:37:34.530
if you're looking for three
nines of availability.

00:37:34.530 --> 00:37:38.820
Notice, in green, we've got your
router in the colo facility.

00:37:38.820 --> 00:37:41.690
Blue, we've got
the Google router.

00:37:41.690 --> 00:37:43.669
Notice there are two
links, not a single link,

00:37:43.669 --> 00:37:45.960
because there's no SLA for
a single-link configuration.

00:37:45.960 --> 00:37:49.140
But we've got two
links configured there.

00:37:49.140 --> 00:37:51.940
And those two links have to
fit specific requirements.

00:37:51.940 --> 00:37:56.640
There are two links that go
into separate physical zones

00:37:56.640 --> 00:37:57.960
within the same metro.

00:37:57.960 --> 00:38:00.360
If you notice, those dedicated
interconnect locations

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:02.430
were defined in a metro area.

00:38:02.430 --> 00:38:04.860
And we use those metro
areas for purposes

00:38:04.860 --> 00:38:08.280
of availability planning, with
maintenance cycles and things

00:38:08.280 --> 00:38:09.520
like that.

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:13.410
So you would have two
links into the same metro,

00:38:13.410 --> 00:38:16.080
but into different physical
zones within that metro.

00:38:16.080 --> 00:38:21.726
And that configuration will give
us three nines of availability.

00:38:21.726 --> 00:38:25.050
If we want to extend
that beyond three nines,

00:38:25.050 --> 00:38:28.170
and we're interested in
four nines of availability,

00:38:28.170 --> 00:38:32.680
what we'd essentially do is
duplicate that configuration.

00:38:32.680 --> 00:38:36.870
Notice now what we've done is
introduce a secondary region.

00:38:36.870 --> 00:38:39.240
So we've got a
similar configuration,

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:41.250
where we've got
multiple links into two

00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:43.380
different physical
zones in the same metro.

00:38:43.380 --> 00:38:47.080
But then we've doubled it in
another region, two more links,

00:38:47.080 --> 00:38:51.210
and to two different physical
zones within a different metro

00:38:51.210 --> 00:38:52.614
in a different region.

00:38:52.614 --> 00:38:54.030
And by configuring
things that way

00:38:54.030 --> 00:38:56.980
because of the additional
geo redundancy,

00:38:56.980 --> 00:39:01.350
this configuration is supported
with four nines of availability

00:39:01.350 --> 00:39:01.920
in the SLA.

00:39:07.650 --> 00:39:12.600
We also discussed that path
from VPN to interconnect,

00:39:12.600 --> 00:39:15.210
which is a common journey
many of our customers take.

00:39:15.210 --> 00:39:17.250
Because sometimes, when
you're just starting out

00:39:17.250 --> 00:39:20.130
with a proof of concept,
or early in the project,

00:39:20.130 --> 00:39:22.380
it's very easy to
stand up a VPN tunnel,

00:39:22.380 --> 00:39:24.480
get that private
connectivity initially going.

00:39:24.480 --> 00:39:27.455
But as that proceeds, as the
initiative gains momentum,

00:39:27.455 --> 00:39:29.580
as you're formalizing your
platform, in many cases,

00:39:29.580 --> 00:39:31.860
you want to follow
that up with something

00:39:31.860 --> 00:39:35.390
like a dedicated interconnect.

00:39:35.390 --> 00:39:39.010
Now, this is going to show us
a way where you can do that,

00:39:39.010 --> 00:39:41.180
and transition
seamlessly from a cloud

00:39:41.180 --> 00:39:44.030
VPN to a dedicated interconnect.

00:39:44.030 --> 00:39:46.850
What we've basically set
up here is a cloud VPN.

00:39:46.850 --> 00:39:47.780
It's pretty standard.

00:39:47.780 --> 00:39:49.340
We've got a couple
of tunnels set up.

00:39:49.340 --> 00:39:52.730
Notice we're using ECMP
routing, as we discussed.

00:39:52.730 --> 00:39:54.230
The only thing
that's really unusual

00:39:54.230 --> 00:39:58.730
here is that we set our
BGP med, or the priority,

00:39:58.730 --> 00:40:03.950
as we call it, to 100 instead
of its default value of 1,000.

00:40:03.950 --> 00:40:05.450
You'll see why that
matters when we

00:40:05.450 --> 00:40:08.180
bring the interconnect online.

00:40:08.180 --> 00:40:10.130
When we bring the
interconnect online,

00:40:10.130 --> 00:40:14.270
we bring it online with its
default priority of 1,000.

00:40:14.270 --> 00:40:19.400
So even as those routes start
to be advertised by GCP,

00:40:19.400 --> 00:40:23.340
the priority 100 routes
are going to be preferred.

00:40:23.340 --> 00:40:26.217
So your traffic is still going
to be flowing over the VPN.

00:40:26.217 --> 00:40:28.550
And then when you're ready
for the interconnect solution

00:40:28.550 --> 00:40:33.020
to go live, you just
reverse the priority values.

00:40:33.020 --> 00:40:35.750
Set the VPN priority to 1,000.

00:40:35.750 --> 00:40:40.700
Set the interconnect
priority to 100.

00:40:40.700 --> 00:40:43.610
And by doing that now,
the interconnect routes

00:40:43.610 --> 00:40:45.380
will be preferred.

00:40:45.380 --> 00:40:46.910
At that point, you
could potentially

00:40:46.910 --> 00:40:50.540
turn the VPN solution
off or, in many cases,

00:40:50.540 --> 00:40:53.690
we'll see customers leave it
there as a backup, a fail-safe.

00:40:53.690 --> 00:40:54.440
It still works.

00:40:54.440 --> 00:40:59.480
If something happened to
the interconnect route,

00:40:59.480 --> 00:41:03.100
the cloud VPN routes would then
be able to service the traffic.

00:41:09.130 --> 00:41:11.130
So we've explored topology.

00:41:11.130 --> 00:41:14.280
We've explored connectivity.

00:41:14.280 --> 00:41:17.160
We also want to explore
some of the security-related

00:41:17.160 --> 00:41:20.430
foundations in GCP.

00:41:20.430 --> 00:41:23.160
One of the common resources
that we're going to use

00:41:23.160 --> 00:41:25.180
are firewalls.

00:41:25.180 --> 00:41:28.527
Now, this slide is showing
us, on the left-hand side,

00:41:28.527 --> 00:41:31.110
a traditional enterprise model
where you might have a firewall

00:41:31.110 --> 00:41:33.630
appliance and you route
traffic through that appliance.

00:41:36.310 --> 00:41:38.410
In the cloud, we prefer
distributed systems.

00:41:38.410 --> 00:41:40.270
We like to avoid those
kinds of chokepoints

00:41:40.270 --> 00:41:41.478
and single points of failure.

00:41:41.478 --> 00:41:44.350
So it's interesting, in GCP,
that firewalls are implemented

00:41:44.350 --> 00:41:45.556
as a distributed system.

00:41:45.556 --> 00:41:46.930
There's not a
firewall appliance.

00:41:46.930 --> 00:41:49.300
You could run a virtual
appliance if you wanted that.

00:41:49.300 --> 00:41:51.760
But GCP's firewalls
are distributed

00:41:51.760 --> 00:41:55.260
enforced on the host, based
on rules that you define.

00:41:58.790 --> 00:42:01.580
And those rules
allow you to specify

00:42:01.580 --> 00:42:05.870
the source of the traffic, the
destination of the traffic,

00:42:05.870 --> 00:42:09.200
protocols and ports.

00:42:09.200 --> 00:42:12.980
Allow or deny that traffic.

00:42:12.980 --> 00:42:16.250
And then target that rule
to a specific instance,

00:42:16.250 --> 00:42:17.390
or set of instances.

00:42:17.390 --> 00:42:19.350
There's a couple
of ways to do that.

00:42:19.350 --> 00:42:24.230
You could do that,
all VMs in a network.

00:42:24.230 --> 00:42:26.250
You could do that
with target tags--

00:42:26.250 --> 00:42:31.350
tags with little text values
that you tag onto the instance.

00:42:31.350 --> 00:42:33.920
You could say web server,
and use that web server

00:42:33.920 --> 00:42:38.690
to target a port 80 or 443 rule.

00:42:38.690 --> 00:42:40.300
You can also target
service accounts.

00:42:40.300 --> 00:42:44.330
Remember, Rae introduced
service accounts as the identity

00:42:44.330 --> 00:42:47.450
that we specify when we
launch that virtual machine.

00:42:47.450 --> 00:42:50.000
So that virtual machine runs
in the context of that service

00:42:50.000 --> 00:42:51.350
account identity.

00:42:51.350 --> 00:42:53.675
And we can actually
target firewall rules

00:42:53.675 --> 00:42:55.050
to that service
account identity,

00:42:55.050 --> 00:42:57.470
or to instances running in
that service account identity.

00:42:57.470 --> 00:42:59.136
And we'll see, on the
next slide, that's

00:42:59.136 --> 00:43:03.410
actually our preferred way
of targeting firewall rules,

00:43:03.410 --> 00:43:07.160
for the reason that
tags are mutable.

00:43:07.160 --> 00:43:09.560
You can change the
tags on an instance.

00:43:09.560 --> 00:43:11.801
And by changing the
tags, you're potentially

00:43:11.801 --> 00:43:13.550
changing the firewall
rules that are being

00:43:13.550 --> 00:43:16.121
enforced on that instance.

00:43:16.121 --> 00:43:18.620
And if we want to be really
sure that the firewall rules are

00:43:18.620 --> 00:43:20.780
defined appropriately and
being used appropriately

00:43:20.780 --> 00:43:22.940
in our environments,
if we target

00:43:22.940 --> 00:43:27.250
service accounts instead,
service accounts have control.

00:43:27.250 --> 00:43:30.410
In IAM, we can
specify which users

00:43:30.410 --> 00:43:33.080
are allowed to launch instances
in which specific service

00:43:33.080 --> 00:43:34.400
accounts.

00:43:34.400 --> 00:43:37.059
And service accounts can't be
changed on a running instance.

00:43:37.059 --> 00:43:38.600
In order to change
a service account,

00:43:38.600 --> 00:43:41.240
you have to stop the instance
first and then start it again.

00:43:41.240 --> 00:43:43.910
And for that reason, if we
target our firewall rules

00:43:43.910 --> 00:43:46.527
to service accounts,
we've got tighter controls

00:43:46.527 --> 00:43:48.110
over the configuration
of those rules,

00:43:48.110 --> 00:43:50.620
and who is allowed to,
say, launch a web server.

00:43:50.620 --> 00:43:52.530
Who is allowed to
launch a server

00:43:52.530 --> 00:43:55.240
you know according
to other rules.

00:44:02.380 --> 00:44:04.600
This slide is introducing
an alpha feature.

00:44:04.600 --> 00:44:06.100
You may or may not
have heard of it.

00:44:06.100 --> 00:44:07.474
There'll be some
discussion of it

00:44:07.474 --> 00:44:11.050
in some of the other
Breakout sessions here next.

00:44:11.050 --> 00:44:14.110
But this is Firewall Logging.

00:44:14.110 --> 00:44:18.130
This is a feature that you
can enable per firewall rule.

00:44:18.130 --> 00:44:20.860
And when it's enabled,
Firewall Logging

00:44:20.860 --> 00:44:22.900
will create a record
of every decision

00:44:22.900 --> 00:44:25.360
when a connection is
either allowed or denied

00:44:25.360 --> 00:44:27.280
because of that rule.

00:44:27.280 --> 00:44:30.850
Firewall Logging is going
to log that decision.

00:44:30.850 --> 00:44:32.920
And then you can
define filters that

00:44:32.920 --> 00:44:36.190
would select all or some of
those firewall logging records,

00:44:36.190 --> 00:44:40.030
and direct them to
Stackdriver logging,

00:44:40.030 --> 00:44:43.120
to Pub/Sub, GCS, BigQuery,
wherever you want

00:44:43.120 --> 00:44:46.420
those logs to be accumulated.

00:44:46.420 --> 00:44:48.370
So this is a great
way to have visibility

00:44:48.370 --> 00:44:51.400
into the security
decisions that are

00:44:51.400 --> 00:44:53.290
being made within
this environment

00:44:53.290 --> 00:44:54.400
that you've defined.

00:45:00.220 --> 00:45:03.220
Another logging feature
that provides visibility

00:45:03.220 --> 00:45:08.230
into our VPC network
environments is VPC Flow Logs.

00:45:08.230 --> 00:45:14.320
Now VPC Flow Logs
provide us a five-second,

00:45:14.320 --> 00:45:18.580
aggregated report for each--

00:45:18.580 --> 00:45:20.300
what we call a 5-tuple flow.

00:45:20.300 --> 00:45:22.900
Now, 5-tuple is a
way that we uniquely

00:45:22.900 --> 00:45:24.730
identify a flow of traffic.

00:45:24.730 --> 00:45:28.270
The 5-tuple is the
source address and port,

00:45:28.270 --> 00:45:32.750
the destination address
and port, and the protocol.

00:45:32.750 --> 00:45:36.600
And that 5-tuple uniquely
identifies the flow of traffic.

00:45:36.600 --> 00:45:40.400
So for each 5-tuple
flow, every five seconds,

00:45:40.400 --> 00:45:42.130
you'll get a log
record that shows

00:45:42.130 --> 00:45:47.710
you the bytes transferred, the
packets, additional annotations

00:45:47.710 --> 00:45:53.170
for the VPCs and the geographic
information of that traffic.

00:45:53.170 --> 00:45:55.300
And again, you can configure
that using Stackdriver

00:45:55.300 --> 00:45:57.670
to filter some or
all of those records,

00:45:57.670 --> 00:46:01.000
and direct them to your
appropriate repository

00:46:01.000 --> 00:46:04.980
to keep those records available
for your analytics queries.

00:46:12.890 --> 00:46:19.080
One final feature that
we'll discuss-- again,

00:46:19.080 --> 00:46:22.739
this is a feature that's
in private beta now.

00:46:22.739 --> 00:46:24.780
And this is an interesting
way-- we talked before

00:46:24.780 --> 00:46:26.320
about the idea of perimeters.

00:46:26.320 --> 00:46:29.820
And as we begin to sort of
segment our environments

00:46:29.820 --> 00:46:32.010
and think about
security perimeters,

00:46:32.010 --> 00:46:34.140
how do we enforce
those perimeters?

00:46:34.140 --> 00:46:40.300
Let's imagine a scenario where
we have created a VPC network.

00:46:40.300 --> 00:46:42.540
We've got VM instances
running in that network,

00:46:42.540 --> 00:46:45.360
and we've allowed
them to egress,

00:46:45.360 --> 00:46:49.380
either via private Google
access or through NAT, or even

00:46:49.380 --> 00:46:50.595
with a public IP address.

00:46:50.595 --> 00:46:53.220
We've allowed them to egress and
interact with our Google APIs,

00:46:53.220 --> 00:46:53.730
right?

00:46:53.730 --> 00:46:56.340
So we've got VMs
running in the VPC,

00:46:56.340 --> 00:46:58.620
and they can interact
with Google Cloud Storage.

00:46:58.620 --> 00:47:00.630
They can write
data into buckets,

00:47:00.630 --> 00:47:03.120
read data from buckets.

00:47:03.120 --> 00:47:06.990
How do we know that the buckets
that those VMs are writing data

00:47:06.990 --> 00:47:10.740
to are actually buckets that
are owned by our company?

00:47:10.740 --> 00:47:13.020
What's preventing
data exfiltration?

00:47:13.020 --> 00:47:16.710
What's preventing a rogue
script from uploading data

00:47:16.710 --> 00:47:20.460
into some bucket that's actually
not owned by our company?

00:47:20.460 --> 00:47:22.920
That's what VPC Service
Controls are for.

00:47:22.920 --> 00:47:26.430
It allows us to define
a service perimeter.

00:47:26.430 --> 00:47:28.290
And that service
perimeter is essentially

00:47:28.290 --> 00:47:31.080
a grouping of
resources, including

00:47:31.080 --> 00:47:36.150
the resources running in our
VPC, the buckets, and other GCP

00:47:36.150 --> 00:47:37.800
resources.

00:47:37.800 --> 00:47:41.160
And it's only within
that service perimeter

00:47:41.160 --> 00:47:42.970
that resources are
allowed to interact.

00:47:42.970 --> 00:47:45.510
So this would, for
instance, make sure

00:47:45.510 --> 00:47:51.660
that instances in our
VPC can interact only

00:47:51.660 --> 00:47:53.670
with company-owned buckets.

00:47:53.670 --> 00:47:57.700
And in fact, instances
in other VPCs,

00:47:57.700 --> 00:47:59.590
or trafficked from
the internet at large,

00:47:59.590 --> 00:48:02.230
would not be able to
interact with those buckets

00:48:02.230 --> 00:48:06.780
because that traffic comes from
outside the service perimeter.

00:48:06.780 --> 00:48:08.530
But you can extend
that service perimeter.

00:48:08.530 --> 00:48:11.530
If you do want to extend
it to allow traffic

00:48:11.530 --> 00:48:14.260
in from the internet, for
web serving scenarios,

00:48:14.260 --> 00:48:15.250
that supported.

00:48:15.250 --> 00:48:18.740
Or if you want to extend it even
to your on-premises network,

00:48:18.740 --> 00:48:21.340
so your data center network
can become part of that service

00:48:21.340 --> 00:48:24.820
perimeter, that's
supported, as well.

00:48:24.820 --> 00:48:27.850
But this tool allows you
to then draw that boundary,

00:48:27.850 --> 00:48:30.700
create that collection
of resources,

00:48:30.700 --> 00:48:33.580
and harden that perimeter.

00:48:33.580 --> 00:48:36.520
And so this will be an
important new feature

00:48:36.520 --> 00:48:39.160
for our network security.

00:48:39.160 --> 00:48:40.930
So hopefully, that
intro has given us

00:48:40.930 --> 00:48:45.661
a feel for some of the design
decisions that we have to make,

00:48:45.661 --> 00:48:47.410
and some of the features
and tools that we

00:48:47.410 --> 00:48:50.560
have in GCP to help you create
your network foundations,

00:48:50.560 --> 00:48:52.831
create your security
foundations.

00:48:52.831 --> 00:48:55.330
Now I want to turn the floor
over to my colleague, Morgante,

00:48:55.330 --> 00:48:57.040
who's going to show
us how to automate

00:48:57.040 --> 00:48:59.350
some of these deployments.

00:48:59.350 --> 00:49:00.161
Thank you.

00:49:00.161 --> 00:49:04.009
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:07.382 --> 00:49:08.340
MORGANTE PELL: Awesome.

00:49:08.340 --> 00:49:08.946
Thanks, Jeff.

00:49:08.946 --> 00:49:10.320
So we spent a lot
of time talking

00:49:10.320 --> 00:49:12.736
today about how you can set
up an effective organizational

00:49:12.736 --> 00:49:14.350
hierarchy and set
up your network.

00:49:14.350 --> 00:49:16.660
But maintaining this over
time can be a lot of work

00:49:16.660 --> 00:49:18.270
if you don't have some
automation in place that

00:49:18.270 --> 00:49:19.980
allows you to enforce
the best practices

00:49:19.980 --> 00:49:21.100
across your organization.

00:49:21.100 --> 00:49:23.040
So every time you're
onboarding new users,

00:49:23.040 --> 00:49:24.979
onboarding new
projects into GCP,

00:49:24.979 --> 00:49:26.520
you can have that
done automatically,

00:49:26.520 --> 00:49:28.350
and have your codified
best practices.

00:49:28.350 --> 00:49:31.059
So putting your automation in
place gives a lot of benefits.

00:49:31.059 --> 00:49:32.850
If it's in source
control, you can actually

00:49:32.850 --> 00:49:33.690
collaborate on it you.

00:49:33.690 --> 00:49:34.814
You can have pull requests.

00:49:34.814 --> 00:49:37.907
You can have suggestions of
changes to policies or changes

00:49:37.907 --> 00:49:39.740
to your network topology,
all collaborative.

00:49:39.740 --> 00:49:41.198
Do a pull request
instead of having

00:49:41.198 --> 00:49:43.877
to only have conversations
or through manual actions.

00:49:43.877 --> 00:49:45.210
You can also ensure consistency.

00:49:45.210 --> 00:49:47.293
You can make sure that
every single project you're

00:49:47.293 --> 00:49:50.060
creating in GCP has the exact
same consistent settings--

00:49:50.060 --> 00:49:52.260
the right subnets, the
right IM permissions,

00:49:52.260 --> 00:49:54.667
the right rules set up for
that to find exactly in code.

00:49:54.667 --> 00:49:56.250
You can also reduce
the manual effort.

00:49:56.250 --> 00:49:58.140
And so having a
single person go in

00:49:58.140 --> 00:50:02.190
and provision a new service
account or a new service

00:50:02.190 --> 00:50:04.370
project, you can just have
the code automatically do

00:50:04.370 --> 00:50:06.990
that in a very consistent
and automatable way.

00:50:06.990 --> 00:50:09.120
And then you can enforce
policies proactively.

00:50:09.120 --> 00:50:11.460
So if you're saying
any of our dev projects

00:50:11.460 --> 00:50:14.190
can only get access
to dev VPC, if that's

00:50:14.190 --> 00:50:17.499
coded into your modules and
into your infrastructure's code,

00:50:17.499 --> 00:50:19.290
you can be confident
that every time you're

00:50:19.290 --> 00:50:21.240
creating those new projects,
they're not accidentally

00:50:21.240 --> 00:50:22.990
getting access to
networks or resources

00:50:22.990 --> 00:50:24.240
they shouldn't have access to.

00:50:24.240 --> 00:50:26.448
So this is what one of the
pipelines might look like.

00:50:26.448 --> 00:50:28.710
You'd have a set of common
modules, or templates,

00:50:28.710 --> 00:50:31.200
for what a project looks
like in your organization,

00:50:31.200 --> 00:50:33.267
or what a network
or subnet looks

00:50:33.267 --> 00:50:34.350
like in your organization.

00:50:34.350 --> 00:50:36.060
And you can combine
them with configs

00:50:36.060 --> 00:50:38.310
specific to a particular
application use case.

00:50:38.310 --> 00:50:40.800
So you could have
a, for example,

00:50:40.800 --> 00:50:44.520
subnetwork that, every
time you were creating

00:50:44.520 --> 00:50:46.664
a subnetwork for
your dev testing,

00:50:46.664 --> 00:50:48.330
that has to have
certain names, and then

00:50:48.330 --> 00:50:50.090
you would have a
service account that's

00:50:50.090 --> 00:50:51.380
creating the firewall rules.

00:50:51.380 --> 00:50:53.046
And that can [INAUDIBLE]
with the config

00:50:53.046 --> 00:50:54.720
specific to the application.

00:50:54.720 --> 00:50:58.050
So you take the configuration
and the modules together,

00:50:58.050 --> 00:51:01.110
combine them into a plan,
and then take that plan,

00:51:01.110 --> 00:51:02.100
and deploy it--

00:51:02.100 --> 00:51:03.210
assuming it looks good--

00:51:03.210 --> 00:51:05.210
to a non-prod environment,
to a dev environment,

00:51:05.210 --> 00:51:06.840
where you can actually
see it deployed.

00:51:06.840 --> 00:51:08.490
See, oh, great, we've
got the right resources,

00:51:08.490 --> 00:51:09.910
we've got the right project.

00:51:09.910 --> 00:51:11.430
And then, once
it's looking good,

00:51:11.430 --> 00:51:15.480
you can actually deploy
that same exact code,

00:51:15.480 --> 00:51:17.130
that same exact
infrastructure's code,

00:51:17.130 --> 00:51:18.820
and promote it into your
production environment.

00:51:18.820 --> 00:51:20.275
So taking that code and put it--

00:51:20.275 --> 00:51:22.400
the same way you have an
application release cycle,

00:51:22.400 --> 00:51:24.274
you can have an
infrastructure release cycle.

00:51:27.420 --> 00:51:29.730
So projects and policies
are some of the things that

00:51:29.730 --> 00:51:31.557
benefit most from this, right?

00:51:31.557 --> 00:51:34.140
So if you stamp up projects in
a very standardized, consistent

00:51:34.140 --> 00:51:36.810
way, you can minimize
the proliferation

00:51:36.810 --> 00:51:38.190
of sensitive owner roles.

00:51:38.190 --> 00:51:41.370
So instead of having to have
individuals going and creating

00:51:41.370 --> 00:51:43.500
projects, in which
case, they, by default,

00:51:43.500 --> 00:51:45.280
get owner on that
new project, you

00:51:45.280 --> 00:51:46.655
can have a service
account, which

00:51:46.655 --> 00:51:48.277
is creating the
projects from code,

00:51:48.277 --> 00:51:50.610
and makes sure that only
people who actually need access

00:51:50.610 --> 00:51:52.210
to the project get access to it.

00:51:52.210 --> 00:51:54.335
And by default, nothing
besides the service account

00:51:54.335 --> 00:51:55.090
has access to it.

00:51:55.090 --> 00:51:57.173
You can also ensure that
you have access to shared

00:51:57.173 --> 00:51:58.910
resources, like the shared VPC.

00:51:58.910 --> 00:52:00.660
Or if you have a common
standardized image

00:52:00.660 --> 00:52:03.545
bucket, where you have
blessed companies base images,

00:52:03.545 --> 00:52:05.670
you can make sure all new
projects, and the service

00:52:05.670 --> 00:52:08.025
accounts in those projects,
get access to that.

00:52:08.025 --> 00:52:09.630
And this can be automated.

00:52:09.630 --> 00:52:12.420
We have Deployment Manager,
Terraform, or our public APIs,

00:52:12.420 --> 00:52:14.520
and your own tooling
using this public APIs.

00:52:14.520 --> 00:52:16.570
So an example flow
for a new project

00:52:16.570 --> 00:52:20.040
might be create the project,
create a Google App Engine

00:52:20.040 --> 00:52:22.830
app in that project, if your
company is using App Engine.

00:52:22.830 --> 00:52:24.780
Enable billing on the
project, and associate

00:52:24.780 --> 00:52:26.363
with the appropriate
billing accounts.

00:52:26.363 --> 00:52:29.490
Enable any allowed APIs
and only the allowed APIs

00:52:29.490 --> 00:52:30.790
on that project.

00:52:30.790 --> 00:52:33.487
And then create specific service
accounts for that project.

00:52:33.487 --> 00:52:35.070
And then finally,
if you actually want

00:52:35.070 --> 00:52:37.020
to have developers, say,
be able to only view

00:52:37.020 --> 00:52:38.610
the logs in that
project, you could

00:52:38.610 --> 00:52:42.489
have the same product factory
process actually set the IM

00:52:42.489 --> 00:52:45.030
policies on the new project,
and give the right people access

00:52:45.030 --> 00:52:47.260
to it.

00:52:47.260 --> 00:52:49.612
So this is talking about
that methodology of how you

00:52:49.612 --> 00:52:50.820
have projects as code, right?

00:52:50.820 --> 00:52:52.736
Instead of projects only
going through the UI,

00:52:52.736 --> 00:52:54.562
you can have an
actual process where

00:52:54.562 --> 00:52:55.770
somebody wants a new project.

00:52:55.770 --> 00:52:58.411
You have a new team that they
want to get access to GCP.

00:52:58.411 --> 00:53:00.660
They're really excited to
start developing their code.

00:53:00.660 --> 00:53:02.940
What they would do is they
could go to central products

00:53:02.940 --> 00:53:04.981
repo, where all the projects
that you want in GCP

00:53:04.981 --> 00:53:05.629
are listed.

00:53:05.629 --> 00:53:07.170
They'd make up their
own branch, make

00:53:07.170 --> 00:53:09.600
some changes to add their
own project listed in there.

00:53:09.600 --> 00:53:12.296
And then make a pull
request that says,

00:53:12.296 --> 00:53:13.420
I want to add this project.

00:53:13.420 --> 00:53:14.920
And then you can
have some automated

00:53:14.920 --> 00:53:17.220
tooling that first looks at
that pull request and says,

00:53:17.220 --> 00:53:17.744
OK, good.

00:53:17.744 --> 00:53:20.160
It's on the right subnets, it
has the right configuration.

00:53:20.160 --> 00:53:22.950
It looks good from the
automated perspective.

00:53:22.950 --> 00:53:25.050
And then have a central
team actually go in

00:53:25.050 --> 00:53:26.730
and manually review
that pull request.

00:53:26.730 --> 00:53:28.639
Make sure that everything
looks good there,

00:53:28.639 --> 00:53:30.930
so that you have both the
automated and the manual sign

00:53:30.930 --> 00:53:31.944
off on it.

00:53:31.944 --> 00:53:33.360
And then once that
all looks good,

00:53:33.360 --> 00:53:35.400
you actually merge that
pull request, right?

00:53:35.400 --> 00:53:37.874
And then, once it's actually
merged into the master branch

00:53:37.874 --> 00:53:40.290
is when it actually gets created
from the service account.

00:53:40.290 --> 00:53:41.956
So no individuals are
creating projects.

00:53:41.956 --> 00:53:44.290
It's always going through
a pipeline through code.

00:53:46.369 --> 00:53:47.910
If you want to learn
more about that,

00:53:47.910 --> 00:53:49.865
we actually have a
section on Thursday

00:53:49.865 --> 00:53:51.240
that's actually
talking about how

00:53:51.240 --> 00:53:54.810
we apply those same
principles of policy as code

00:53:54.810 --> 00:53:57.450
to Kubernetes, and taking
those same principles of a pull

00:53:57.450 --> 00:54:00.460
request workflow for
changing your policies.

00:54:00.460 --> 00:54:04.266
So this works really well
for proactive defense, right?

00:54:04.266 --> 00:54:06.390
If you have policies that
are defined through code,

00:54:06.390 --> 00:54:08.431
and you're proactively
finding [? any ?] problems

00:54:08.431 --> 00:54:10.246
as you're creating
new infrastructure,

00:54:10.246 --> 00:54:11.370
that's really great, right?

00:54:11.370 --> 00:54:13.830
But there can be
drift over time.

00:54:13.830 --> 00:54:15.810
There might be some legacy
organization admins.

00:54:15.810 --> 00:54:18.030
There may be some
different services running.

00:54:18.030 --> 00:54:20.650
GCP sometimes even
changes certain things.

00:54:20.650 --> 00:54:22.740
For example, you're active
in Kubernetes Engine,

00:54:22.740 --> 00:54:25.410
that will create some service
accounts on your project.

00:54:25.410 --> 00:54:28.110
So you also want to
have reactive visibility

00:54:28.110 --> 00:54:31.015
and detection of any
policy violations.

00:54:31.015 --> 00:54:33.390
That's where you have an open
source tool called Forseti,

00:54:33.390 --> 00:54:35.130
which can actually
do that violation

00:54:35.130 --> 00:54:37.680
detection retrospectively.

00:54:37.680 --> 00:54:40.640
It works by, first,
building inventory.

00:54:40.640 --> 00:54:43.579
So it has an inventory of all
the resources, all the way down

00:54:43.579 --> 00:54:45.120
to the individual
VM or firewall rule

00:54:45.120 --> 00:54:47.700
level within your
entire organization.

00:54:47.700 --> 00:54:49.830
And it takes that inventory
and it can actually

00:54:49.830 --> 00:54:51.150
run policy analysis over it.

00:54:51.150 --> 00:54:52.250
So you can say, all right.

00:54:52.250 --> 00:54:54.810
Here's all the firewall
rules in my organization.

00:54:54.810 --> 00:54:57.600
I should have no firewall
rules that target IP ranges,

00:54:57.600 --> 00:55:01.200
because we decided the best
practice that firewall rules

00:55:01.200 --> 00:55:02.870
only target service accounts.

00:55:02.870 --> 00:55:05.070
So you could have that
as one of your policies,

00:55:05.070 --> 00:55:08.280
and Forseti could flag any
rule that violates that policy.

00:55:08.280 --> 00:55:10.190
And then in some cases--

00:55:10.190 --> 00:55:12.870
in all cases where there's
a policy violation,

00:55:12.870 --> 00:55:13.620
would notify you.

00:55:13.620 --> 00:55:15.561
So notify administrators
with an email.

00:55:15.561 --> 00:55:18.060
But also, it could actually
even automatically enforce that.

00:55:18.060 --> 00:55:20.100
So bring it back into
a compliant state,

00:55:20.100 --> 00:55:22.467
into compliance with
your code policies,

00:55:22.467 --> 00:55:23.550
in a really effective way.

00:55:23.550 --> 00:55:25.560
So that works with GC
firewalls, and we're

00:55:25.560 --> 00:55:27.364
adding more over time.

00:55:27.364 --> 00:55:29.280
And there's also a Forseti
section on Thursday

00:55:29.280 --> 00:55:30.930
if you guys are interested
in checking that out,

00:55:30.930 --> 00:55:32.596
and diving really
deep into what Forseti

00:55:32.596 --> 00:55:35.187
can do to help you have
automated policy analysis.

00:55:37.602 --> 00:55:39.810
So we're talking a lot about
how you can automate all

00:55:39.810 --> 00:55:41.670
these things together, right?

00:55:41.670 --> 00:55:43.170
Obviously, Google supports this.

00:55:43.170 --> 00:55:44.900
We want you to be able to have
your infrastructure defined

00:55:44.900 --> 00:55:47.640
in code, which is why we have
Google Deployment Manager.

00:55:47.640 --> 00:55:50.610
It's our hosted native tool
for creating our infrastructure

00:55:50.610 --> 00:55:52.140
as code.

00:55:52.140 --> 00:55:53.280
It's a hosted service.

00:55:53.280 --> 00:55:55.800
So there is a UI for it, same
as any of our other projects.

00:55:55.800 --> 00:55:57.175
You can go into
the cloud console

00:55:57.175 --> 00:55:59.258
and find information about
the Deployment Manager,

00:55:59.258 --> 00:56:00.800
see all of your
existing deployments,

00:56:00.800 --> 00:56:03.008
and even see what the status
of those deployments is.

00:56:03.008 --> 00:56:04.440
So what resources
did the create,

00:56:04.440 --> 00:56:07.140
what configuration values were
passed into those deployments?

00:56:07.140 --> 00:56:09.515
And one thing that's really
cool about Deployment Manager

00:56:09.515 --> 00:56:10.870
is it's totally API-driven.

00:56:10.870 --> 00:56:12.827
It's not custom logic
for each individual API

00:56:12.827 --> 00:56:13.910
that it's integrated with.

00:56:13.910 --> 00:56:17.027
It's using the same public APIs
that you see in our API docs

00:56:17.027 --> 00:56:18.360
to actually call those services.

00:56:18.360 --> 00:56:20.675
The really big advantage
of that is when--

00:56:20.675 --> 00:56:21.800
you're here at Next, right?

00:56:21.800 --> 00:56:23.490
You're hearing about people
coming out with new features

00:56:23.490 --> 00:56:26.150
for all of their different
products all the time.

00:56:26.150 --> 00:56:28.087
When nodes are added
to an existing API,

00:56:28.087 --> 00:56:29.670
the Deployment Manager
can immediately

00:56:29.670 --> 00:56:31.195
get support for
those new features

00:56:31.195 --> 00:56:33.070
because it's just calling
the underlying API.

00:56:33.070 --> 00:56:35.490
So if there's a new property
added to a particular API,

00:56:35.490 --> 00:56:37.991
it will immediately be able to
call that API and get access.

00:56:37.991 --> 00:56:39.906
So you're not being
time-boxed, and you're not

00:56:39.906 --> 00:56:41.340
having to wait
for the automation

00:56:41.340 --> 00:56:42.900
to catch up with
where you're at,

00:56:42.900 --> 00:56:45.420
which is a huge benefit to
being able to adopt automation

00:56:45.420 --> 00:56:47.940
across the board for all
of your infrastructure.

00:56:47.940 --> 00:56:49.590
And templates, of course--

00:56:49.590 --> 00:56:51.472
back to when we first
started talking,

00:56:51.472 --> 00:56:53.180
we want to have these
all be templatized.

00:56:53.180 --> 00:56:55.310
You want to have things
in a very consistent way.

00:56:55.310 --> 00:56:58.540
You're not custom writing config
for every single deployment you

00:56:58.540 --> 00:56:59.040
want to do.

00:56:59.040 --> 00:57:01.469
You want to have reusable
templates across the company.

00:57:01.469 --> 00:57:02.760
Those can be written in Python.

00:57:02.760 --> 00:57:04.644
So really expressive,
full language.

00:57:04.644 --> 00:57:06.060
You've got all the
power of Python

00:57:06.060 --> 00:57:08.170
to create your templates
through Deployment Manager.

00:57:08.170 --> 00:57:10.128
But if you want something
a little bit simpler,

00:57:10.128 --> 00:57:11.730
there's also Jinja2
templates, which I

00:57:11.730 --> 00:57:13.021
can show some examples of here.

00:57:15.639 --> 00:57:16.492
Cool.

00:57:16.492 --> 00:57:18.700
So this is an example of
some of the deployment types

00:57:18.700 --> 00:57:19.600
that we have.

00:57:19.600 --> 00:57:22.000
So on the left here is if
you wanted to do that project

00:57:22.000 --> 00:57:22.870
creation hierarchy.

00:57:22.870 --> 00:57:24.760
We were talking about how you
want to stamp out products

00:57:24.760 --> 00:57:25.990
in a very consistent way.

00:57:25.990 --> 00:57:28.040
This is how you do it with a
Python template in Deployment

00:57:28.040 --> 00:57:28.510
Manager.

00:57:28.510 --> 00:57:30.634
So notice here, we've taken
a couple of parameters.

00:57:30.634 --> 00:57:34.180
We've taken a product ID, a
project name, and a folder ID.

00:57:34.180 --> 00:57:36.490
So we say we want to put
this into our dev folder,

00:57:36.490 --> 00:57:38.575
for example, and we
want to create that.

00:57:38.575 --> 00:57:40.450
We can take those
parameters in, and actually

00:57:40.450 --> 00:57:42.902
have Deployment Manager,
through this common template,

00:57:42.902 --> 00:57:44.860
do the correct association,
create that project

00:57:44.860 --> 00:57:45.871
in the correct folder.

00:57:45.871 --> 00:57:47.620
And actually, even
here-- it's at the end,

00:57:47.620 --> 00:57:48.759
it's a little bit cut off--

00:57:48.759 --> 00:57:50.800
you can even do the billing
accounts association.

00:57:50.800 --> 00:57:52.690
So create that project
and then attach it

00:57:52.690 --> 00:57:55.464
to the correct billing account
as part of that template.

00:57:55.464 --> 00:57:57.130
Now, if you want to
go a little bit more

00:57:57.130 --> 00:57:59.650
advanced-- you're going really
deep into service accounts,

00:57:59.650 --> 00:58:01.430
and doing really
advanced custom rules,

00:58:01.430 --> 00:58:03.431
and you're pushing
the box and you

00:58:03.431 --> 00:58:05.680
want to have a very clear
definition of what a service

00:58:05.680 --> 00:58:07.221
account, what
permissions it is-- you

00:58:07.221 --> 00:58:08.765
can start doing IM custom roles.

00:58:08.765 --> 00:58:10.390
So if you want to
create a custom role,

00:58:10.390 --> 00:58:11.960
that can be done through the UI.

00:58:11.960 --> 00:58:14.140
But again, we want to think
about things as policy.

00:58:14.140 --> 00:58:16.040
We want these policies
to be in code, right?

00:58:16.040 --> 00:58:17.620
So if it's in code,
it's much easier

00:58:17.620 --> 00:58:19.300
to know what that
role is and change

00:58:19.300 --> 00:58:20.980
the permissions associated
with that role over time.

00:58:20.980 --> 00:58:22.355
So that's why, on
the right here,

00:58:22.355 --> 00:58:24.130
we have an example
of a Jinja template

00:58:24.130 --> 00:58:26.110
for creating a custom
role, where we've

00:58:26.110 --> 00:58:28.600
taken some properties
within the curly braces

00:58:28.600 --> 00:58:31.485
there, like a role ID, a
title for their role, some

00:58:31.485 --> 00:58:33.790
of the permissions that
are included in that role.

00:58:33.790 --> 00:58:36.410
All through the curly
braces there on the side.

00:58:36.410 --> 00:58:38.410
Now, what's really cool
about Deployment Manager

00:58:38.410 --> 00:58:40.970
templates is we want them
to be reusable, right?

00:58:40.970 --> 00:58:43.540
So you want to switch them
between multiple contexts.

00:58:43.540 --> 00:58:46.360
That's why, as you can see
here, there is the ENV part.

00:58:46.360 --> 00:58:48.680
It can actually pick
up the current project

00:58:48.680 --> 00:58:52.100
from the current context by
referencing the ENV part there.

00:58:52.100 --> 00:58:54.220
That allows you to take
that same template,

00:58:54.220 --> 00:58:56.050
run it against
different projects,

00:58:56.050 --> 00:58:58.120
and actually have the
those roles be created

00:58:58.120 --> 00:58:59.780
in the correct current project.

00:58:59.780 --> 00:59:01.240
So it allows a
lot of flexibility

00:59:01.240 --> 00:59:04.519
to actually have these
templates adapt over time.

00:59:04.519 --> 00:59:06.310
So we've gotten a lot
of examples of these.

00:59:06.310 --> 00:59:08.590
If you go on GitHub,
we've published dozens,

00:59:08.590 --> 00:59:10.180
and we're publishing
more every day,

00:59:10.180 --> 00:59:12.513
of samples of how you use
Deployment Manager to automate

00:59:12.513 --> 00:59:14.630
these things.

00:59:14.630 --> 00:59:17.450
Now Google, of course, wants to
make sure that we have a hosted

00:59:17.450 --> 00:59:19.460
and supported solution
for this, but we also

00:59:19.460 --> 00:59:21.060
embrace the open
source community.

00:59:21.060 --> 00:59:22.670
We know there are other
tools out there that

00:59:22.670 --> 00:59:25.169
can support all these tools,
which is why we even have teams

00:59:25.169 --> 00:59:27.020
at Google that
work on maintaining

00:59:27.020 --> 00:59:30.122
really strong support for
open source tooling in GCP.

00:59:30.122 --> 00:59:31.580
So Terraform is an
example of this,

00:59:31.580 --> 00:59:33.200
where we want to
make sure there's

00:59:33.200 --> 00:59:36.300
a really strong integration
between Terraform and GCP,

00:59:36.300 --> 00:59:39.260
so that over time, you can very
easily use Terraform to define

00:59:39.260 --> 00:59:41.390
all of your
infrastructure and have

00:59:41.390 --> 00:59:42.682
it be configured automatically.

00:59:42.682 --> 00:59:45.181
So this is an example of how
you could use a service account

00:59:45.181 --> 00:59:45.960
credentials file--

00:59:45.960 --> 00:59:48.980
that's a JSON credentials file
that you download from GCP--

00:59:48.980 --> 00:59:52.597
to authenticate Terraform
to access our APIs.

00:59:52.597 --> 00:59:54.930
And you configure the project
that it's accessing there.

00:59:54.930 --> 00:59:56.555
And then down below,
we see an example

00:59:56.555 --> 00:59:58.954
of how you create a project
in Terraform, right?

00:59:58.954 --> 01:00:00.620
So you would say this
is the project ID,

01:00:00.620 --> 01:00:02.161
this is the billing
account ID that I

01:00:02.161 --> 01:00:05.252
want to attach that project
to, an org ID, and then a name

01:00:05.252 --> 01:00:05.960
for that project.

01:00:05.960 --> 01:00:08.241
And that can be done very,
very easily in Terraform.

01:00:08.241 --> 01:00:10.490
And one of my personal things
that I really like to do

01:00:10.490 --> 01:00:12.330
is actually have bindings
declared in code.

01:00:12.330 --> 01:00:14.230
So you don't have people
going manually and saying,

01:00:14.230 --> 01:00:16.022
oh, I want project
admin, and somebody just

01:00:16.022 --> 01:00:17.480
does that randomly,
and nobody even

01:00:17.480 --> 01:00:18.830
knows if they granted that role.

01:00:18.830 --> 01:00:20.871
You can actually do it
all through Terraform code

01:00:20.871 --> 01:00:23.980
here, where you have a Google
project IM binding saying,

01:00:23.980 --> 01:00:25.610
the people defined
in this role--

01:00:25.610 --> 01:00:27.150
in this case, Jane
at example.com,

01:00:27.150 --> 01:00:29.950
get the project editor
role on that project.

01:00:29.950 --> 01:00:31.740
And it can be done
totally in code.

01:00:31.740 --> 01:00:33.781
And one of the things that
this actually will do,

01:00:33.781 --> 01:00:36.330
with the project IM binding,
is wipe out any other policies.

01:00:36.330 --> 01:00:38.600
So if somebody went in and
manually gave themselves

01:00:38.600 --> 01:00:40.890
editor, but they're not
defined in the Code,

01:00:40.890 --> 01:00:44.180
the next time the code is run,
that binding will be removed.

01:00:44.180 --> 01:00:46.027
It makes sure that
only the people who

01:00:46.027 --> 01:00:47.860
are supposed to have
the role have the role,

01:00:47.860 --> 01:00:49.430
and that you always do it
through the code instead

01:00:49.430 --> 01:00:50.304
of doing it manually.

01:00:54.717 --> 01:00:56.300
So one of the things
Jeff talked about

01:00:56.300 --> 01:00:58.640
was that really,
our best practice

01:00:58.640 --> 01:01:00.770
for having secure
firewall rules is do them

01:01:00.770 --> 01:01:01.904
through service accounts.

01:01:01.904 --> 01:01:04.320
You don't want to have people
going in and editing network

01:01:04.320 --> 01:01:07.340
tags, and allowing their VM
to egress wherever it wants,

01:01:07.340 --> 01:01:08.660
ingress wherever it wants.

01:01:08.660 --> 01:01:10.940
You want to have some
security controls over who

01:01:10.940 --> 01:01:12.630
can do certain firewall rules.

01:01:12.630 --> 01:01:15.735
So this is an end-to-end example
of how you could do that.

01:01:15.735 --> 01:01:17.360
Up here in the
top-left, you'd actually

01:01:17.360 --> 01:01:18.620
just create a service account.

01:01:18.620 --> 01:01:20.400
You'd give it an
account ID, and you'd

01:01:20.400 --> 01:01:23.120
give it a display name
for that service account.

01:01:23.120 --> 01:01:24.674
And then down below,
you'd actually

01:01:24.674 --> 01:01:26.840
do a binding of who can
access that service account.

01:01:26.840 --> 01:01:29.210
Who can take the service
account user role,

01:01:29.210 --> 01:01:31.680
and is able to launch VMs
with that service account?

01:01:31.680 --> 01:01:34.160
So this is saying,
only our web app devs

01:01:34.160 --> 01:01:36.770
are allowed to launch service
accounts using the web app

01:01:36.770 --> 01:01:37.970
service account.

01:01:37.970 --> 01:01:41.120
So another team that is
not authorized to use that

01:01:41.120 --> 01:01:42.632
can't go and create
VMs because they

01:01:42.632 --> 01:01:44.090
don't have the
service account user

01:01:44.090 --> 01:01:45.690
role on that service account.

01:01:45.690 --> 01:01:48.304
So this gives us access control
of who can actually do it.

01:01:48.304 --> 01:01:50.220
And then finally, you'd
have the firewall role

01:01:50.220 --> 01:01:51.917
defined to allow
ingress on port 80

01:01:51.917 --> 01:01:54.500
for that service account, which
gives you a lot of flexibility

01:01:54.500 --> 01:01:56.710
that you don't want anybody to
be able to open their service

01:01:56.710 --> 01:01:57.470
account--

01:01:57.470 --> 01:01:59.330
their VM up to the internet.

01:01:59.330 --> 01:02:01.220
You only want people
who have that role,

01:02:01.220 --> 01:02:03.000
and have that binding
defined, to be

01:02:03.000 --> 01:02:06.390
able to actually allow internet
ingress into their service.

01:02:10.127 --> 01:02:12.710
So now, I just want to skip into
a demo, where we can actually

01:02:12.710 --> 01:02:14.390
show you guys how
some of this works.

01:02:21.100 --> 01:02:22.660
So here's my Terraform config.

01:02:22.660 --> 01:02:24.904
We're going to be using
Terraform today to show off

01:02:24.904 --> 01:02:25.779
some of the features.

01:02:25.779 --> 01:02:27.210
I just want to
show how I actually

01:02:27.210 --> 01:02:29.980
have my Terraform back-end
state stored in GCS.

01:02:32.727 --> 01:02:35.310
And then, here's how we actually
can define our whole resource

01:02:35.310 --> 01:02:37.380
hierarchy within Terraform.

01:02:37.380 --> 01:02:39.349
So we want to have
multiple folders.

01:02:39.349 --> 01:02:41.640
In this case, we're doing a
split between prod and dev.

01:02:41.640 --> 01:02:43.710
So we define our
prod folder up here.

01:02:43.710 --> 01:02:46.320
And we define a dev
folder down here.

01:02:46.320 --> 01:02:48.820
And then, we have
an existing folder.

01:02:48.820 --> 01:02:50.220
Maybe we have a web team.

01:02:50.220 --> 01:02:53.350
They've got all their dev
resources within that folder.

01:02:53.350 --> 01:02:55.339
They can apply policies
to that folder.

01:02:55.339 --> 01:02:57.630
Now, let's say we want to
add an additional folder just

01:02:57.630 --> 01:02:58.740
for our demo today.

01:03:01.250 --> 01:03:06.200
So we're doing some
demo at Next '18.

01:03:06.200 --> 01:03:09.410
So we could do demo.

01:03:09.410 --> 01:03:10.374
Demo dev 18.

01:03:10.374 --> 01:03:12.290
And we want to keep it
in the dev fold, right?

01:03:12.290 --> 01:03:15.710
So it's still a nested folder
underneath the dev folder.

01:03:15.710 --> 01:03:18.260
So this is all I need to
do to add a new folder.

01:03:18.260 --> 01:03:19.580
It's all defined in code.

01:03:19.580 --> 01:03:21.913
And theoretically, if this
was a production environment,

01:03:21.913 --> 01:03:24.080
I'd be doing pull
requests for these parts.

01:03:24.080 --> 01:03:25.337
So I have that folder created.

01:03:25.337 --> 01:03:27.170
And then I actually
want to create a project

01:03:27.170 --> 01:03:28.410
within that folder.

01:03:28.410 --> 01:03:31.220
So this is where I take
this standardized config

01:03:31.220 --> 01:03:32.350
that I have.

01:03:32.350 --> 01:03:34.370
So this is a module that
was built for Terraform

01:03:34.370 --> 01:03:35.960
that allows you to
create a project

01:03:35.960 --> 01:03:38.810
and attach it to a
bunch of resources

01:03:38.810 --> 01:03:40.470
and, actually, create a new one.

01:03:40.470 --> 01:03:44.690
So I'm going to do a new file,
copy this over, and customize

01:03:44.690 --> 01:03:46.040
it for my new project.

01:03:46.040 --> 01:03:51.050
So in this case, I want to
call it, let's say, demo.

01:03:51.050 --> 01:03:59.040
And I want to call it our
demo app, just for today.

01:03:59.040 --> 01:04:00.980
And then, we want to put
it in that new folder

01:04:00.980 --> 01:04:01.938
that we created, right?

01:04:01.938 --> 01:04:06.300
So that folder was Dev Demo.

01:04:06.300 --> 01:04:08.107
And then, you often
want to have groups.

01:04:08.107 --> 01:04:09.690
So one of our best
practices is to use

01:04:09.690 --> 01:04:11.440
groups for access management.

01:04:11.440 --> 01:04:13.050
And as part of
the standard tool,

01:04:13.050 --> 01:04:15.180
you can actually even
create the groups for you.

01:04:15.180 --> 01:04:18.430
If you didn't already
have the groups available,

01:04:18.430 --> 01:04:20.400
you still want to get
the initial editors

01:04:20.400 --> 01:04:22.259
for the project in a group.

01:04:22.259 --> 01:04:24.300
This can actually even
create that group for you.

01:04:24.300 --> 01:04:26.710
So we could say
demo devs, and we're

01:04:26.710 --> 01:04:28.794
going to give them the
editor role in the project.

01:04:28.794 --> 01:04:30.293
We've got some sort
of configuration

01:04:30.293 --> 01:04:31.890
here of what billing
account to use,

01:04:31.890 --> 01:04:34.590
what credentials file to use
for [? acquitting ?] everything.

01:04:34.590 --> 01:04:36.880
And then even an API
service account group.

01:04:36.880 --> 01:04:39.730
Now, my first project
only used Compute.

01:04:39.730 --> 01:04:42.600
Let's say I also want to
use Pub/Sub on this one.

01:04:42.600 --> 01:04:48.660
So I just add Pub/Sub in here
as an additional declaration.

01:04:48.660 --> 01:04:50.580
And I could have
exactly what APIs

01:04:50.580 --> 01:04:53.220
are going to be activated
on the new project defined

01:04:53.220 --> 01:04:54.790
right here in my code.

01:04:54.790 --> 01:04:56.530
And then, finally, shared VPC.

01:04:56.530 --> 01:04:57.420
We talked a lot
about how you want

01:04:57.420 --> 01:04:59.200
to make sure that service
accounts get access

01:04:59.200 --> 01:05:00.200
to the right shared VPC.

01:05:00.200 --> 01:05:04.802
So here, I specify which
product is my shared VPC host.

01:05:04.802 --> 01:05:06.510
This is a service
project we're creating,

01:05:06.510 --> 01:05:08.640
and we want to specify
what the host product is.

01:05:08.640 --> 01:05:09.750
But then, even down--

01:05:09.750 --> 01:05:11.670
we can granularity down
to the subnet level.

01:05:11.670 --> 01:05:14.800
So we don't share all the
subnets from that host project.

01:05:14.800 --> 01:05:16.200
We just want to
share, let's say,

01:05:16.200 --> 01:05:20.100
our US central non-prod
network, our non-prod subnet.

01:05:20.100 --> 01:05:22.470
So this defines that exactly
that subnet is shared,

01:05:22.470 --> 01:05:24.270
and no other ones.

01:05:24.270 --> 01:05:27.099
Rae mentioned labels as one of
our really effective mechanisms

01:05:27.099 --> 01:05:28.890
for getting additional
granularity and info

01:05:28.890 --> 01:05:30.919
on how your projects work.

01:05:30.919 --> 01:05:32.960
So you can actually specify
some labels for this.

01:05:32.960 --> 01:05:36.880
So let's say it's demo app.

01:05:36.880 --> 01:05:38.560
And then, this is
all the config I

01:05:38.560 --> 01:05:40.606
need to do to actually
create that new product.

01:05:40.606 --> 01:05:41.980
And then I might
want to actually

01:05:41.980 --> 01:05:45.410
add an output, just to get back
the project ID when I'm done.

01:05:45.410 --> 01:05:53.380
So I could just do
demo, and demo here.

01:05:53.380 --> 01:05:54.039
Cool.

01:05:54.039 --> 01:05:55.330
So we've got on our new config.

01:05:55.330 --> 01:05:56.500
Everything is looking good.

01:05:56.500 --> 01:05:58.291
Presumably, this would
go through some sort

01:05:58.291 --> 01:06:00.970
of merge request process,
so I do some pull requests.

01:06:00.970 --> 01:06:02.650
And then, as part of
that pull request,

01:06:02.650 --> 01:06:04.660
somebody would run
a Terraform plan.

01:06:04.660 --> 01:06:09.824
So I do Terraform plan.

01:06:09.824 --> 01:06:11.490
So actually, first,
I do a [INAUDIBLE],,

01:06:11.490 --> 01:06:13.323
which will actually
take care of downloading

01:06:13.323 --> 01:06:14.680
the modules from GitHub.

01:06:14.680 --> 01:06:16.530
So you saw that this one was
actually hosted on GitHub.

01:06:16.530 --> 01:06:18.946
So it will go out, fetch the
modules, fetch the providers,

01:06:18.946 --> 01:06:20.667
and actually pull everything in.

01:06:20.667 --> 01:06:22.000
And then I can actually plan it.

01:06:26.700 --> 01:06:28.710
So this acquires a state
lock, checks everything

01:06:28.710 --> 01:06:31.200
that currently exists.

01:06:31.200 --> 01:06:34.230
Doing a bunch of checking.

01:06:34.230 --> 01:06:37.350
And then you can see, here's all
the changes that can be made.

01:06:37.350 --> 01:06:39.090
I'm going to create
a new project.

01:06:39.090 --> 01:06:41.160
Here is the new
product being created.

01:06:41.160 --> 01:06:42.490
And do a bunch of other
things in the process.

01:06:42.490 --> 01:06:43.830
I'm going to create
the new folder.

01:06:43.830 --> 01:06:44.880
So we have to create
the folder before we

01:06:44.880 --> 01:06:46.310
create the new project.

01:06:46.310 --> 01:06:49.740
We have to attach that new
project to our shared VPC.

01:06:49.740 --> 01:06:54.034
We have to grant the
API service account.

01:06:54.034 --> 01:06:55.700
Google always has an
API service account

01:06:55.700 --> 01:06:57.267
associated with
every project that

01:06:57.267 --> 01:06:59.850
is used for any time Google is
doing something on your behalf.

01:06:59.850 --> 01:07:01.410
So for example, a
manage instance group--

01:07:01.410 --> 01:07:02.890
whenever [INAUDIBLE] the
manage instance group

01:07:02.890 --> 01:07:05.390
is putting up new VMs, we go
through the API service account

01:07:05.390 --> 01:07:07.610
that Google created.

01:07:07.610 --> 01:07:10.260
And we want to associate
that to the VPC subnets.

01:07:10.260 --> 01:07:11.510
So [INAUDIBLE] shared.

01:07:11.510 --> 01:07:14.180
We want to make sure the group
that has access to this project

01:07:14.180 --> 01:07:15.740
also has access to
use those subnets

01:07:15.740 --> 01:07:17.239
that you're sharing
to that project.

01:07:17.239 --> 01:07:18.570
So we do a role there.

01:07:18.570 --> 01:07:20.912
And then we do a service
account role to VPC subnets,

01:07:20.912 --> 01:07:23.120
so we make sure that the
service that's being created

01:07:23.120 --> 01:07:24.680
gets access to the subnets
that are being shared.

01:07:24.680 --> 01:07:25.580
Because there's a lot
of these different--

01:07:25.580 --> 01:07:27.800
if you want to have those
very granular permissions,

01:07:27.800 --> 01:07:28.850
you have to make
sure that everything

01:07:28.850 --> 01:07:30.350
that needs to have
access to subnets

01:07:30.350 --> 01:07:32.660
does have that level of access.

01:07:32.660 --> 01:07:35.150
And then, as I mentioned, you
actually can even have it--

01:07:35.150 --> 01:07:37.100
this automation can even create
the G Suite group for you.

01:07:37.100 --> 01:07:38.750
So it'll create
the G Suite group

01:07:38.750 --> 01:07:41.570
and give it the editor
role in that project.

01:07:41.570 --> 01:07:44.174
And then, it's going to create
a default service account,

01:07:44.174 --> 01:07:46.340
so you have one service
account to get started with.

01:07:46.340 --> 01:07:48.673
You can add more service
accounts into the configuration

01:07:48.673 --> 01:07:49.760
if you wanted to.

01:07:49.760 --> 01:07:51.569
And then do some
membership there.

01:07:51.569 --> 01:07:53.360
So I'm just going to
go ahead and run this.

01:07:56.517 --> 01:07:57.600
I just do Terraform apply.

01:08:01.587 --> 01:08:02.420
That's just running.

01:08:05.330 --> 01:08:07.689
Say yes.

01:08:07.689 --> 01:08:09.730
And it's going to go ahead
and create everything.

01:08:09.730 --> 01:08:11.810
So it figures out how to parse
things in the correct order.

01:08:11.810 --> 01:08:13.580
So it knows to actually
create the folder

01:08:13.580 --> 01:08:15.200
before it creates the new
project because, obviously,

01:08:15.200 --> 01:08:17.359
the folder has to exist
for the project to work.

01:08:17.359 --> 01:08:19.310
And it's going ahead
and spinning everything

01:08:19.310 --> 01:08:20.340
up over time.

01:08:20.340 --> 01:08:23.600
So it's creating the project,
doing the subnet association,

01:08:23.600 --> 01:08:24.660
doing everything there.

01:08:24.660 --> 01:08:26.430
One thing I do want to call
out while it's doing that,

01:08:26.430 --> 01:08:29.000
is something that's pretty
special about how this works.

01:08:29.000 --> 01:08:31.880
It actually will take
that API service account

01:08:31.880 --> 01:08:33.750
and put it into a group for you.

01:08:33.750 --> 01:08:38.689
Because by default, you can have
a proliferation of many service

01:08:38.689 --> 01:08:40.609
accounts within
your organization.

01:08:40.609 --> 01:08:43.580
Every project you create
has some service accounts,

01:08:43.580 --> 01:08:45.979
and has multiple service
accounts for different APIs.

01:08:45.979 --> 01:08:47.569
So there's a lot of
benefit to actually having

01:08:47.569 --> 01:08:49.020
groups, that group
those different service

01:08:49.020 --> 01:08:49.770
accounts together.

01:08:49.770 --> 01:08:51.429
So if you wanted to
say all my service

01:08:51.429 --> 01:08:52.887
accounts in the
organization should

01:08:52.887 --> 01:08:57.200
be able to pull GCE images
from a common project,

01:08:57.200 --> 01:08:59.409
you can just give them a
role at the group level.

01:08:59.409 --> 01:09:00.950
So the same way we
talked about users

01:09:00.950 --> 01:09:03.859
being roles, service accounts
can also be placed into groups.

01:09:03.859 --> 01:09:05.149
So that's one thing
this does, is actually

01:09:05.149 --> 01:09:06.969
takes that newly created service
account and all the service

01:09:06.969 --> 01:09:08.593
accounts on that
project, and puts them

01:09:08.593 --> 01:09:10.264
into a group for
easy administration.

01:09:13.229 --> 01:09:16.845
So it takes a while for this
to actually process everything.

01:09:16.845 --> 01:09:22.200
So I'm just going to
speed it up a little bit.

01:09:22.200 --> 01:09:23.609
My magic little speed-up.

01:09:27.520 --> 01:09:30.500
And at the end here, you can
see we actually have the output.

01:09:30.500 --> 01:09:34.660
So for our new GCP
project, you can actually

01:09:34.660 --> 01:09:38.250
see GCP Next '18,
simple demo 18.

01:09:38.250 --> 01:09:40.149
That's our new project
that was created.

01:09:40.149 --> 01:09:43.960
If I wanted to go over here
into the project hierarchy,

01:09:43.960 --> 01:09:47.350
I can actually go ahead
and find my folder.

01:09:47.350 --> 01:09:53.660
So Next '18 Dev, Demo 18,
and my app was created.

01:09:53.660 --> 01:09:57.716
And we see it actually even has
half of the shared networks.

01:09:57.716 --> 01:09:59.590
So it did that shared
[INAUDIBLE] attachment.

01:09:59.590 --> 01:10:00.714
Everything's ready to go.

01:10:00.714 --> 01:10:02.380
And this team is now
onboarded and ready

01:10:02.380 --> 01:10:04.540
to start using GCP in
this standard policy

01:10:04.540 --> 01:10:05.982
config, standard project.

01:10:05.982 --> 01:10:08.607
They have all the resources they
need to start getting forward.

01:10:08.607 --> 01:10:09.106
Cool.

01:10:11.395 --> 01:10:12.770
So that's the demo
we have today.

01:10:12.770 --> 01:10:15.080
I'll welcome Peter-Mark
up to close out.

01:10:15.080 --> 01:10:18.014
[APPLAUSE]

01:10:23.400 --> 01:10:26.910
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: So just
to reiterate where we started

01:10:26.910 --> 01:10:31.950
and where we began this talk,
I showed you this migration

01:10:31.950 --> 01:10:33.900
methodology that we
use with our customers

01:10:33.900 --> 01:10:37.110
when we're trying to migrate
large-scale environments

01:10:37.110 --> 01:10:40.200
in data centers and
so on, from assess,

01:10:40.200 --> 01:10:43.590
to foundation,
deploy, and optimize.

01:10:43.590 --> 01:10:47.460
And we've really zeroed
in on the foundation side.

01:10:47.460 --> 01:10:52.230
And that's because I think
it's so important for customers

01:10:52.230 --> 01:10:56.370
to actually spend the
time, spend the energy

01:10:56.370 --> 01:10:57.330
focusing on this part.

01:10:57.330 --> 01:11:00.420
Because really, the amount of
time that you invest upfront

01:11:00.420 --> 01:11:03.030
in those first two stages in
particular-- when you plan out,

01:11:03.030 --> 01:11:04.500
you find out everything
that you have,

01:11:04.500 --> 01:11:05.999
you find out what
you want to move--

01:11:05.999 --> 01:11:07.680
and then you create
this foundation

01:11:07.680 --> 01:11:11.260
of what you need to move to
the cloud is so important.

01:11:11.260 --> 01:11:14.670
It really is such a metric for
success for the later stages

01:11:14.670 --> 01:11:16.120
of migration.

01:11:16.120 --> 01:11:18.360
We really feel it's
worthwhile spending time

01:11:18.360 --> 01:11:21.004
to focus in on this, and
find out what you need to do.

01:11:21.004 --> 01:11:22.920
And then for us to come
out here and do things

01:11:22.920 --> 01:11:24.900
like this, where we
talk about what we think

01:11:24.900 --> 01:11:28.650
are the best ways to
do things like this.

01:11:28.650 --> 01:11:31.430
There are a couple of sessions
that might be interesting

01:11:31.430 --> 01:11:35.210
if you'd like to see
other sessions where

01:11:35.210 --> 01:11:38.150
people talk about migrations
they've gone through.

01:11:38.150 --> 01:11:43.740
These four sessions are to come
in the next couple of days,

01:11:43.740 --> 01:11:45.890
and I highly recommend them.

01:11:45.890 --> 01:11:49.430
Unfortunately, a couple of
sessions have already passed,

01:11:49.430 --> 01:11:50.840
but all of our
sessions are going

01:11:50.840 --> 01:11:53.330
to be on YouTube in
a couple of weeks.

01:11:53.330 --> 01:11:54.860
These ones would
be worth looking up

01:11:54.860 --> 01:11:57.070
if you'd like to watch,
again, other people's

01:11:57.070 --> 01:11:59.240
stories about the
things they've done.

01:11:59.240 --> 01:12:04.190
And in particular, I think
the Velostrata session,

01:12:04.190 --> 01:12:05.880
and the Cloud Sprint session.

01:12:05.880 --> 01:12:07.910
Those are both
Google led sessions.

01:12:07.910 --> 01:12:10.130
Velostrata, we
recently acquired,

01:12:10.130 --> 01:12:11.750
they do VM migrations.

01:12:11.750 --> 01:12:15.230
And Cloud Sprint is our program
by our professional services

01:12:15.230 --> 01:12:18.680
organization that
comes into customers

01:12:18.680 --> 01:12:22.220
who are beginning a
migration journey,

01:12:22.220 --> 01:12:28.190
and helps them kick that off by
creating a well-secured, agile,

01:12:28.190 --> 01:12:30.560
best practices cloud foundation.

01:12:30.560 --> 01:12:33.280
And on that note,
thank you very much.

01:12:33.280 --> 01:12:35.240
All four of us will be
happy to take questions.

01:12:35.240 --> 01:12:36.740
[APPLAUSE]

01:12:36.740 --> 01:12:40.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

